const e="escaramuza-charra-mexico",d=2026,t="Built from the FMCH 2024-2028 regulations (edition February 2026): Libre category and the Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil and Charras Mayores document, both read in full (compared line by line with the 2021-2024 Libre regulation that the template cited). The template cites the 2021-2024 regulation; the newer 2024-2028 values are used (for example the 290-point minimum for a state champion pass, 52 maximum additional points, the National pass for only the earlier year's champion, the -2 penalty for a calzonera that shows the leg) and the older text is not copied where it was replaced. The regulation prints no entry fees, prize amounts, dates, venue addresses, phone numbers or e-mail addresses; the Secretary of Sport and coordinator names are printed with no contact details. Birth years for Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B and Juvenil are set each year by the Secretariat of Sport and are not printed. The template group Infantil covers Dientes de Leche, Infantil A and Infantil B; its age ranges (Mayor 40 to 99) differ from the regulation (Charras Mayores 45 and older). Description of the attire is summarised garment by garment; the long lists of forbidden colours and materials are kept. The regulation's judging chapters for the judges themselves (article numbers 62 to 69) and the delegate and caporal duties are captured under Page key 'general' and 'judging'.",a=JSON.parse(`{"Title":"Rules & Criteria","Subtitle":"Escaramuza Charra (Mexican women's synchronized team riding): official regulation 2024-2028 of the Mexican Charreria Federation (FMCH). Read these rules before you register.","NoticeTitle":"Please Read Before Registering","Notice":"Only federated teams of eight women riders can compete. Before each state championship the captain gives the delegate the printed documents 24 hours ahead. A wrong or missing item of attire or tack can disqualify the team, so check the regulation. The regulation is published at {{FmchWebsite}}.","Notes":["The team is always eight women riding side-saddle (a mujeriegas) on the albarda saddle. Mixed teams and men are not allowed.","Categories: Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil, Libre and Charras Mayores (45 years and older).","Each rider needs a valid FMCH card for the sports year and the FMCH major medical expenses insurance.","Documents must be filled in black ink on the official sheets. Late or wrong papers cost -6 points.","The routine is judged on 12 exercises (6 in Dientes de Leche) at the gallop, with traditional Mexican music."],"Sections":[{"Key":"general","Title":"General Rules and Roles","Items":["The Escaramuza Charra is the sport that shows the skill of the rider and the horse, in a group of 8 horse and rider pairs (binomios).","Its historical frame is the keeping of the dress and traditions of Charreria. The riders ride the horses with the charro rein style.","Together, the 8 riders show a Compulsory Competition Routine (Rutina Obligatoria de Competencia) at the gallop.","An escaramuza is made up only of female riders. Mixed teams (by gender) and men in the group are not allowed, because the riders sit side-saddle on the albarda.","The FMCH wants its events to keep the customs, traditions and charro work, and to keep the classic dress of the rider and her horse in its purest form.","The attire and tack are judged in escaramuza competitions and official events. They must be worn properly and complete even when not competing.","This regulation replaces all earlier official regulations. Changes caused by changes to the FMCH statute, the general competition regulation or other sports rules take effect when they are published at {{FmchWebsite}}.","Not knowing the regulation is not an excuse for captains, instructors, relatives, companions and judges. A fault by any of them is penalised on the team they belong to, train or accompany, or on them if they are affiliated, under this regulation and the FMCH statute.","Riders, instructors and judges must keep to what the regulation says, both to compete and to judge. Everyone works with honesty and professional ethics.","At the tack check, if an element does not meet the regulation and would mean disqualification, the team gets a warning and must correct it before its next official competition. Disqualification is avoided only if the item shown is traditional and/or charro. If the team does not correct it and the same remark appears at its next official competition, the team is penalised by the regulation.","A competition is a sports contest between three or more escaramuza teams (Libre teams of the same state in the Libre category). It starts when the team has its terrain recognition at the official competition and ends when the captain signs the score sheets.","Official competitions are the ones called by the National Board of the FMCH: the Congress and National Charro Championship; the National Charro Championship for Children, Youth and Escaramuzas (Infantil, Juvenil and de Escaramuzas); the National Championship of Charros Mayores; State Championships 1 and 2; Regional Championships; the Revolution National Tournament; the Federation Anniversary National Tournament; the Constitution National Tournament; the Independence Championship; and the National Games (called with the National Commission of Physical Culture and Sport).","Official competitions are organised by the official host venues, judged by members of the National College of Judges under the current Official Competition Regulation, and run under the Sports Rules published by the Secretariat of Sport and endorsed by the FMCH.","Friendly competitions are judged by their call. What the call does not cover is judged by the Official Regulation for Escaramuzas and Damas Charras and the FMCH statute. What cannot be solved by the regulation is solved by those who call the competition.","Teams must be federated and always compete with eight riders.","From the moment a team confirms in writing that it will take part in an official competition, it is bound by this regulation, the statute, the agreements and specifications circulated by the FMCH, and the call.","A team must compete before the ring doors are closed after the coleadero (steer tailing). When justified, teams can also compete in escaramuza fairs.","In escaramuza fairs and State Championships, one judging day can have at most 10 teams in one block per day. With more than 10 teams, the teams are split over the days needed.","Official competitions are held in a charro arena (lienzo charro): 100 metres long, with a lane (manga) of 60 metres and a ring (ruedo) of 40 metres diameter.","In the ring, a centred rectangle of 6 metres wide by 20 metres long is drawn between 70 and 90 metres, without the line at the entrance of the rectangle. The halves and eighths must be marked on the ring wall, fixed and visible. The halves and eighths are white; if the contrast is poor, they can be a more visible colour.","A circle of 9 metres radius is marked as a tool to measure the dimensions of the exercises, only in the Juvenil, Libre and Charras Mayores categories. It must be marked in all official competitions.","The FMCH, the organising committees and their directors, judges and members do not take responsibility for accidents or damage of any kind to riders, horses, grooms or other people who take part directly or indirectly in the competitions. The organising committees must provide the medical and veterinary services needed for first aid.","A team that must delay its presentation because of an accident can be allowed by the judge to take part in the same event at a time the judge decides, with the Sports Commission. The timer and the score restart when the team restarts. If it repeats an exercise already done, the penalties for it stay and everything that happens is penalised again.","Nobody (delegate, team, sponsor, relative or trainer) can object to the assignment of judges. A team that does is disqualified.","When the papers reach the FMCH offices, the National Escaramuza Coordination makes a second review of the infractions. If a penalty is marked but the points were not added in the infractions box, or the infraction points are more than the marked penalties, the score is corrected and can go up or down.","The delegate (delegada) helps the good running of official competitions and is responsible for making sure that everything in the call and the agreements of the Secretariat of Sport is followed.","To be a state escaramuza delegate: her post lasts as long as the president of the state Union agrees; she must be a federated member of an association of her state, defined in sport in the state she is in charge of; she is an assistant to the PUA (president of the Union of Associations), has no authority and must report all activity organised in the state; she must have more than three years as a member of the FMCH in an escaramuza of her state; she can keep riding in her state and be a captain; she must know the statute and the escaramuza competition regulation well; and she must live in the state during her post.","Delegate duties: give the Escaramuza Coordination a calendar annual work plan; attend all FMCH meetings in Mexico and/or the USA; register the state's teams in time, with the payment receipt and the signatures of the PUA, the charro association president, the captain, the delegate and the instructor; check that teams and riders get their registration and payment documents and cards on time; check before official competitions that teams are federated; serve all teams without distinction; guide captains in paperwork; wear the traditional attire; not charge for her work.","Delegate duties at events: when a team has no valid cards at its state championship, tell the captain to bring photo ID and the payment receipt or deposit slip, with the delegate's approval signature, for the responsible judge; ask the Escaramuza Coordination for judges one month before the events (state seminar and state championships) and agree with them the arrival and return dates; plan the logistics by number of teams (order of participation, a detailed report, the places of the judges, the official terrain recognition times); be present at the terrain recognition; ask teams for the official papers; give the judges the list of teams with name and category for the first and second state championship, the medical check proof and the report of official practice.","The delegate asks the host association of the state championship for: wall marks of halves and eighths; medical service (ambulance) from the practice; the official FMCH male judges to measure the puntas; announcers; sound with at least two wireless microphones for the responsible judge and the male judge.","The delegate names a Sports Commission to check that the terrain is good and that the recognition schedule is followed (the delegate assists the commission); makes sure there are closed-circuit TV and two cameras for recording and video review with sound (a DVR replay system is recommended; tablets or phones are not allowed for recording official competitions or video review); makes sure videos are given to the responsible judge on CD and/or USB at the end of the event; and sets a private place for giving the scores (recorded with sound; the recording goes to the responsible judge on the same day).","The delegate names a person who is not a judge or the delegate to run the video and copy the sheets. The delegate is never inside the video review. She names a caporal for women's competitions. She treats the judges with courtesy, makes sure the hosts pay the judges' fees, hotel, food and transport and the courier fee for sending the scored sheets and videos to the FMCH, plans the number of days the judges stay, has a copier at official events, and gives the delegate's attendance list of captains at the seminar to the judge at the start of a competition (and for State Stage 2 the list of teams that competed in Stage 1).","The delegate must not come close to the judges while they score or give scores, and must not act to favour or harm any team. She gives captains on time a list of all the expenses of each official event, proves them to the teams or their representatives, and sends a copy to the FMCH and the National Coordinator of Escaramuzas.","The caporal (female) must know the regulation fully, dress as the regulation says, report to the judge for instructions, be present and mounted at least one hour before the competition, invite captains to register and go on time to the medical check, check that the arena is ready (reference points marked, rectangle and circle painted, a 2-metre lime line at 10 metres from the partidero along the side walls for the straight-line bonus, official judges for measuring the puntas present, lane and ring clear, sound and announcers ready, a separation line 8 metres from the rectangle for press and announcers).","The caporal also gathers the teams for tack check, organises the parade on time, gives the entry signal to the first punta rider and later the second, checks that the rectangle is cleaned and the ring is empty before the eight enter, cannot take her own decisions without the judge's authority, tells the judge about any incident, and is the contact between the judge and the riders. At the end of the last team's performance she enters the centre of the ring to show the competition is over. She cannot ride puntas or ride sides in the ring or lane while on duty and cannot compete in the event she serves. If she acts wrongly or in bad faith against a rider or team, she is removed and warned.","Official FMCH male judges (jueces varones) measure the puntas. The judge on the ground who measures can disqualify a rider who disrespects him, and her team if the disrespect comes from the fans, friends, relatives or trainer, and he must do it at the moment of the disrespect.","Only press and media staff authorised by the FMCH Press and Dissemination Secretariat, with a badge, can be at the edge of the ring or lane (behind the walls) after telling the jury. Being inside the arena, lane or ring during the routine is a reason to disqualify the team. During puntas they stay behind a line at least 8 metres from the rectangle lines and out of the rider's line of sight."]},{"Key":"eligibility","Title":"Eligibility","Items":["Only women can compete in an escaramuza. The escaramuza is made up only and exclusively of female riders.","A team is formed by eight riders. A team with more or fewer than eight riders at the moment of competing is disqualified.","A team must be federated. Not being federated is a reason to disqualify the team.","Only riders with a valid FMCH card for the current sports year can take part in official competitions. No other document replaces it.","If a rider has no original FMCH card, she can show official photo ID (voter card, school card, vaccination card, passport and so on) and the payment of her fee.","All riders must have the major medical expenses insurance offered by the FMCH.","A rider who belongs to two or more federated escaramuza teams defines her sports activity at her first official event of the year, with the escaramuza, association and state she will officially compete for. If she competes in another team and/or state of the same category, the second team is disqualified.","Riders can belong to one or more federated associations but must define themselves as a competitor for only one. A rider can compete in another team and/or state in a different category, but she must be federated in both teams and both states.","A team can change state without penalty if it has written permission of entry and exit from the presidents of the Unions of Associations (PUA) involved and from the FMCH, and keeps the call.","In State Championships, only for reasons of force majeure, a team can compete in a state other than the one where it is federated, to look for its score to qualify. It cannot compete for the prizes or be State Champion. It must show the written permissions to the judges and the coordination, or it is not scored.","The captain must ride with her escaramuza. If she does not, she cannot do any procedure.","The instructor must be federated.","Instructors, captains, riders and fans must not make malicious comments or insult another team during training or during the competition, or the team is disqualified.","Nobody can compete with injured or drugged horses, or with riders who are drugged, drunk or under the effect of a substance that changes their performance. A rider who arrives with alcohol on her breath or drunk is disqualified.","A rider who competes with a cast or splint on any part of the body without a written medical authorisation does it at her own risk. If the rider gives a responsibility letter that frees the FMCH of all responsibility, there is no penalty.","Tattoos on view or hair dyed an unnatural colour (pink, green, purple, blue and so on) disqualify the rider.","Piercings or objects in perforations on any visible part of the body other than the earlobe cost -2 points.","Official competitions are open to the team that has been in the state championship Stage 1 of its state; Stage 2 is only for teams that took part in Stage 1; the Regional Championship is only for teams that took part in State Stages 1 and 2 (attending is not required).","A rider who is a queen of her association, state or the nation and whose team competes the same day can parade as queen in her participation dress.","The categories for Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B and Juvenil are made up of riders born in the years set by the Secretariat of Sport for the sports year.","Younger riders of other categories can support a team of a higher age without limit of number. The oldest rider decides the category of the team. An infantil rider or team can compete in a higher category at its own risk. A rider of a higher category can never compete in a lower category.","Girls and young riders (Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil) must define themselves and compete in only one of these categories in all official competitions, from State Championships to the annual National Infantil, Juvenil and Escaramuzas Championship. They cannot take part in two or more of these categories in these championships.","Infantil and Juvenil riders can reinforce only one escaramuza of the Libre category if they wish, even if they competed or will compete in the Infantil or Juvenil category during the year, in the same team, in a different association or in another state.","The Charras Mayores category is for riders 45 years old and older. Younger riders cannot take part even if they are adults. The team can have riders of the same or different states.","All Infantil competitors must ride in albarda and side-saddle. For safety, in no category can riders be tied to the saddle with thongs, sashes or any other object that holds them to the horse or saddle."]},{"Key":"registration","Title":"How to Register and Papers","Items":["The captain gives the required documents for any official FMCH championship on time and in the right form, or the team gets -6 points. Time is set by the regulation or the call of the tournament. The judge sets the time to correct rejected papers according to the registration times.","Right form: official score sheets (Hojas de Calificacion) and sketch and responsibility sheets (Hojas de Croquis y Responsiva) in the official formats, filled in all their spaces, valid FMCH cards, the papers asked for, and the official consent form for minors.","Only official score sheets and sketch and responsibility sheets, of the official size (oficio), that the Federation sends through its delegates and are on {{FmchWebsite}}, are accepted. The drawings of the exercises are printed or by hand, all in black ink. If not, the team is disqualified.","The drawing of each exercise must show the exact moment of the cuaje (crossing). It must be clear, simultaneous and with intersection, nose-to-tail or in T depending on the exercise concept, between the riders who do the exercise.","For State Championships 1 and/or 2, captains give their delegate the printed documents 24 hours before the event for the judges to check. After that the captain can no longer change them or she gets -6. Any remark by the jury is written on the sheets already given.","Documents (1): three official score sheets with all required information.","Documents (2): four official sketch and responsibility sheets with all required information. On the back of one of them are the photocopies of the eight valid cards of the riders, of the instructor, and of the substitute if there is one. Without original FMCH cards, show an official photo ID for each rider and a copy of the payment receipt and/or deposit.","Documents (3): a responsibility letter in the official format, signed in original (no copies) by the father, mother or guardian (when needed).","Documents (4): the music of the performance on USB or CD.","For the Infantil, Juvenil and Charras Mayores categories (reglamento 2024-2028): original birth certificates with copies and/or a printed CURP.","The documents must be complete (original responsibility letters, copy of the cards on the back of the sketch sheet for riders and trainer, or proof of payment of affiliation). If not, the penalty is -6.","For national championships, the captain sends the documents asked for in the call to the FMCH offices in time and in the right form, or gets -6. If the team needs to correct its papers it must do it the day before its competition.","Teams that are told by the National Coordination must confirm their participation in the Congress and National Championship in writing on the date in the call. If not, they give up the right to go, and their place goes to the next team of the general list.","The captain can make the changes she thinks necessary in riders and in exercises, with prior notice to the judges.","A team must show the card or photo ID of each of the eight riders at the tack check. The team is disqualified if it does not show the original cards or the ID. A team that alters a card is disqualified and the judge writes a report signed by herself and the delegate, sent to the Secretariat of Sport and then to the Honour and Justice Committee for punishment.","A captain who does not attend the annual theoretical seminar of her state, dressed as Adelita or charra, cannot present a refutation letter in any competition of that sports year. The same applies to the team of an instructor who does not attend. If there is no FMCH call to the trainers' seminar, the trainer attends the captains' seminar.","Medical check: in official FMCH competitions the escaramuzas must pass the medical review when the service is available. If not, -6.","In a state, if teams of the same association are two or more, they take part independently in their State Championship Stage 1, Stage 2 and Regional Championship. To go to the National Championship they can exchange riders as long as they compete with the team name that earned the pass.","A team must come on time to the tack check or it gets -6 and its tack is checked after its performance or when the judges decide."]},{"Key":"levels","Title":"Rounds and Advancement","Items":["The classification of escaramuzas begins when the team competes in the State Championship of the state where it is federated at the start of the sports year. Pre-state competitions are optional because they are not official events. If held, they must be open to anyone who wants to take part, and their score counts for the general classification.","In states with at least three teams, they can compete among themselves. The Libre State Champion gets a direct pass to the National Charro Championship if she has a minimum score of 290 points and competed with 8 riders.","The score for the State Champion of each state is the average of the two State Championships. For the other teams, the highest score of State 1 or State 2 is used, with a minimum of 290 points. All teams must attend both State Championships to get the higher score. A team that does not attend the second championship has its State 1 score voided and loses its right to the National Championship of that year. This holds if at least 3 teams of the same category and state took part. The scores of the other teams go to the General Classification Table.","A team that has a pass to the National earned at an earlier official event does not need to attend State 1 or 2. It can compete only for the prize if any, or for the state champion plaque. If it wins, the pass goes to the team in the next qualifying place.","Teams that earned a pass in the earlier National Championship must attend an official event called by the FMCH to keep the pass. They must compete with the same team name that earned the pass and at least 5 of the riders who competed and won the pass.","State Stage 2 championships are only for teams that took part in State Stage 1. Regional Championships are only for teams that took part in State Stage 1 and 2, and attending is not required.","Regional Championships are held in 7 regions of Mexico. Larger regions have two direct passes to the National Charro Championship of the year and smaller regions have one. The regions are set in each call. A Regional Championship is held only if the region has at least 50% plus 1 of its total teams taking part. They are held only in Mexico.","The Libre State Champion of each state has a direct pass to the Congress and National Championship with a minimum average score of 290 points and with 8 riders.","Direct passes to the National Championship: the National Champion of the earlier year (she must attend an official FMCH championship to keep her pass); all State Champions with at least 290 points; the National Champion of the United States of America of the current year; the Independence Championship of Charreria (as the call says); the Regional Championship (as the call says); by the position table; and direct passes given by the FMCH from official tournaments. The National Board of the FMCH decides the number of teams at the National Championship.","Infantil and Juvenil path: Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B and Juvenil teams must compete with 8 riders in their State Stage 1 to have the right to compete in the National Charro Championship for Children, Youth and Escaramuzas of that year. State Stage 2 is optional for them.","If an Infantil team cannot go to State Stage 1 because of proven force majeure (for example, taking part in the National Games), its case goes to the Secretariat of Sport for authorisation so as not to lose the right to compete in the Infantil National.","A Juvenil team that wants to go to the Congress and National Charro Championship (Libre) as well as the National Infantil and Juvenil Championship must take part in State Stage 1 and Stage 2, with the same duties and rights as Libre teams, and it can become Libre State Champion. It takes part only once in the State Championships and the score counts for both categories.","The Charras Mayores category does not have to go to any state championship. With the requirements of the call in time and form, it has the right to compete in the National Charros Mayores Championship. The category competes only in the Charras Mayores National Championship."]},{"Key":"teams","Title":"Teams, Captain and Instructor","Items":["A competition escaramuza team has eight riders.","Captain: the team names a captain to represent it before the Federation. She gives the documents the regulation asks for and represents the team in all procedures. She must ride with her team.","The captain attends the annual seminar called by the FMCH early in the year, dressed properly. If not, her team loses the right to present a refutation letter.","The captain fills in her sheets completely, drawing her exercises as well as possible. The drawing must be clear, simultaneous and with intersection. In turns she marks the entry and exit.","The captain checks her sheets at the end of the video review to see that the sums are correct. She asks for explanations of her score during the video review, with full knowledge of the cause and respect for the judges.","Captains, riders, relatives, instructors and people close to a team cannot ask for explanations or question another team's score. The team is disqualified if they do.","The captain must accept the judges' decision. If she disagrees with her score, she writes it in the part of the score sheet meant for it. That space cannot be used for other teams. If a team wants to send a complaint it can send a letter to the Secretary of Sport, signed by its captain, with proof.","A captain or anyone related to the team who is disrespectful to the jury causes the disqualification of the team, which is sent to the proper bodies, with other penalties if the person is affiliated with the FMCH.","A captain who arrives with alcohol on her breath or in an improper state cannot receive the score, and her team is disqualified, since she is the only person who can receive the score.","An instructor is any person who gives practical advice on foot or on horse, long or short, paid or free, and may or may not ride with the team defined for the sports year.","The instructor must be federated and must attend the annual seminar called by the FMCH early in the year, dressed properly. If not, the teams she trains lose the right to present a refutation letter.","The instructor follows this regulation and the FMCH statute, including in the competition process of her team. Only the instructor's card is photocopied.","An instructor cannot give instructions to the team during the punta or the competition. If she does, the punta or the exercise where it happened is disqualified. She cannot be present at the delivery of the team's scores or step in at it (Libre).","An instructor must not make negative comments about the jury during the competition, or take part for or against any other team, or the team(s) are disqualified from the competition. Any incident goes in the warning format and is sent to the proper bodies. The trainer who causes disrespect to the male or female judges or creates controversy in any state at any time is penalised.","Substitutes: a substitute rider's card is also copied on the sketch sheet."]},{"Key":"conduct","Title":"Conduct","Items":["A team is disqualified for: wearing attire or tack not allowed in the regulation; altering the attire, tack and horses by mixing categories; riding in escaramuza competitions in the gran gala or etiqueta charra suit or the China Poblana suit; competing in a second team in the same category after being defined in another; not being federated; not having taken part in State Championship 1 and wanting to take part in State 2; having more or fewer than eight riders when competing; opposing the judges assigned by the FMCH; leaving a competition without finishing it; altering an FMCH card of any of the eight riders; disrespect to the jury by the captain or anyone related to the team (the team then cannot close its sheets); negative remarks about judges, teams or scores by the instructor during the competition; presenting more punta than allowed; not stopping completely before the first routine exercise; being late for its puntas; giving a gift or throwing objects of any kind at any time; riders, friends, relatives, grooms or instructors throwing any object from the stands to the ring or arena; not meeting the compulsory concepts, the values asked or the allowed base sums; using any part of the horse braided, decorated, tails tied or gathered to the saddle, false pieces or any paint; not showing original FMCH cards or a photo ID at tack check; using a gargantón in competition; adding applications other than leather, hide or suede, or unspecified drawings or landscapes on the saddles; hardware of the saddle with zirconia, pearls and so on; the instructor not properly dressed when checking the punta measure.","A rider is disqualified for: not wearing any part of the attire and tack allowed for competitions; not wearing a crinoline under the Adelita dress; visible tattoo or unnatural dyed hair; alcohol on the breath or drunkenness; short calzoneras showing above the boot; glued chapetas (not fixed with screws or staples).","Offending or insulting any other escaramuza during training or the competition by riders, friends, relatives, accompanying people or trainers causes the team's disqualification.","Escaramuzas, riders, instructors and judges must keep to this regulation. They work with honesty and ethics.","No gifts, awards or objects may be handed to the team or thrown into the ring or arena at any time.","Conduct complaints: unsporting conduct is any verbal or physical action that goes against the spirit of the sport, respect for the competition, the judges' work, the participating teams or the organisation of the event. It gets a formal warning from the proper authority. Faults are recorded and told to the captain, trainer, escaramuza team or judge involved. If it happens again, a written notice with a copy to the proper commission is given. If the conduct goes on, the Honour and Justice Commission decides the penalty, from a warning to temporary or permanent suspension, by the gravity of the faults.","The Coordinator, the state and regional delegates and the judges are monitored and evaluated to make sure they do their duties with professionalism."]},{"Key":"judging","Title":"Judging and the Jury","Items":["The jury of official competitions is made up of trained Damas Charras assigned by the National College of Judges of the FMCH, with their institutional appointment.","To be an official FMCH judge: be a woman and an adult; have at least five years of experience competing in escaramuzas and be active; be an active member of an association of the FMCH for at least two years; have an endorsement letter from the charro association where she is federated; pass the admission exam; do the theoretical and practical training and pass it; pass the riding exam; take all the courses she is told to take; be objective, honourable, fair and impartial; show personal and professional ethics; pass the exam given each year to be allowed to judge; and send a written request to the National College of Judges, promising to respect the statutes and regulations. She cannot represent or train any escaramuza team while she is in the College of Judges. Her actions are written in a personal record book (carnet).","The number of FMCH judges is unlimited, but none can judge at official events unless the National Board approves her appointment each year.","Judges are divided into State Judges, Regional Judges and National Judges. State judges are women who know the competition regulation and are provisionally authorised by the Union president and the section coordinator of the National College of Judges, then approved or voided by the National Board. Regional judges are state judges approved by the National Board for a region. National judges meet the requirements, have been state and regional judges, passed the practical and theoretical exams, and are appointed each year by the National College and ratified by the National Board.","Official judges are the highest authority in the competitions. They solve any contingency with the statute and the Official Regulation. Their decision must be respected and cannot be argued or appealed, and stands above any order from a hierarchy.","The FMCH backs the function of judges as long as they are federated and have passed the update course of the current year. Events judged by non-official judges, or judges not updated at the FMCH, have no backing from the Institution. It is forbidden to accept to judge with a non-official judge at official events; she can be dismissed.","There are two judges in Stages I and II of the State Championship. For other official events, the Secretariat of Sport and the Judges Coordinator set the number of judges and the rota. Only the National Judges Coordinator assigns the official judges, endorses the male judge(s) who measure puntas, and is the only authority to name the responsible judge of the event. She can also assign practising judges.","No judge or jury can judge a state championship if her duties cause a conflict of interest. Judges must tell the Coordinator and the Secretariat of Sport of invitations to judge unofficial events as soon as they accept. They must tell the FMCH of any conflict of interest when accepting an official or unofficial event, or be warned.","All costs of judges' training are paid by them. The costs of going to judge officially are paid by the host or the FMCH according to the current tariff.","Judges are warned for unsporting conduct, aggression, arrogance, dishonesty, not knowing the regulation, wrong use of mobile phones or personal radios while judging, breaking the Honour Code, not keeping the times of the review and competition process, and so on. After three warnings the case goes to the Honour and Justice bodies, which decide a temporary or permanent suspension.","Judges check the papers in official format with printed copies at least two hours before the competition starts (if there is no notice about paper delivery) and all the judges who will judge the event check them. Judges arrive two hours before the event. They tell the delegate their remarks on the sheets, or else they tell the captain. They tell the captain if the team wears tack or attire that is not allowed and/or different, if they detect it before the competition, and if non-official score sheets are being used, so that they are corrected. They also tell the captain of detectable errors in the routine movements when receiving the papers.","With two judges, a central judge is placed at the middle of the stands and another at the middle right or left, depending on the arena. In arenas with high stands, the jury sits at the middle height, both at the same level. If there are no stands, the delegate gives the facilities.","The central judge scores the puntas, with the help of the male judges and the second judge, who is in the lane of the ring. If they disagree on any point they can ask to check the video with a male judge.","Judges score at the moment and do not close their sheets; they only add horizontally. They review the video in front of the captain, correct the sums, and when the sheet is reviewed and signed by judge and captain, the score is official. Judges wait for the captains up to half an hour after the team's participation. If the captain does not come in that time, judges no longer have to explain the sheet, and the captain only signs.","The judges and the captain write their names clearly and sign the score sheets. The score is official when the captain and the jury sign it. If the captain does not sign, the score does not become official and she cannot sign at any other time, even if the competition has not ended. This is a lack of respect for the competition process: the participation and the official rights for the current year are voided as unsporting conduct, and the judge makes a report sent to the Secretariat of Sport.","Judges can only show or lend score sheets to nobody, before, during or after the competition, and must not comment on the scores. A breach is sent to the proper bodies with a warning.","Judges must send the Final Official Scores report at once by electronic means to the National Coordinator, the Secretariat of Sport and the FMCH Press and Dissemination Secretariat. The responsible judge fills the report format and attaches it to the scored sheets of the teams, and sends them to the FMCH in at most five working days after the event, or gets the warning.","A judge can keep riding in her state. She cannot be the captain of her team.","Judges' Honour Code: the judge is the highest authority in the arena; she wears the traditional dress with purity; she gives no shows that go against morals, does not drink alcohol during competitions or official events while dressed as Adelita or charra; she scores with impartiality; she does not accept to take part in official competitions without authorisation from the National College; does not promote herself to be invited; does not represent the College without authorisation; attends the seminars and passes the exam; never asks for or accepts payment to give extra score or harm a team; answers the captain with respect and clarity; lets the captain write a disagreement by hand and signed, and promises to send it to the Escaramuza Coordination even if she is involved; does not let strangers stand next to her; applies the current regulation at all times; takes part in the official events the FMCH asks; does not judge events where the FMCH regulation is not kept; her ruling is above any hierarchical order; knows that her case goes to the Honour and Justice Committee if she breaks these rules; takes no part in political campaigns and asks for leave if needed.","Video review: it takes place when the judges meet the captain. The captain decides what to review and in what order. The team has 15 minutes of review with both judges (10 minutes in Dientes de Leche), counted without stopping the timer, except when changing camera, video or memory card, or moving on to the next judge.","The captain asks the judge what she wants to review and decides when to move to the other part of the video. The judges first tell the captain the error and then check it in the video, never the other way round. (In Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B and Juvenil, the captain and her instructor do this together.)","Only the penalties the captain decided to review are checked. The penalties marked on the score sheet must match in concept and position, or the penalty does not stand, for the benefit of the team. Defects are taken off only once per exercise. The judge must show that the marked rider is the one who made the error (an individual mark), and prove it on video. If several riders are marked together, the captain asks the judge to point to the rider before reviewing the video.","If a marked penalty is not proved on video, or does not match in concept and position, the judge does not apply it and corrects the official sheet in red ink without subtracting it.","If a judge marks a collision, a refusal, a disqualification for any reason, not completing the marked degrees of the turns, anything that leads to a direction change, bonus points, or not meeting the Compulsory Routine, and it is proved on the official videos, the other judge(s) must mark it in red and subtract it in front of the captain (only these errors are handled this way). The penalties that happened as a consequence of the disqualification and after it are removed from the sheet.","If after the horizontal sums are closed a penalty is found that is not marked in its box, the judge can no longer add it, except for a collision, a refusal, a disqualification for any reason, not completing marked degrees, a direction change, bonus points or not meeting the Compulsory Routine, if marked and proved by another judge.","The judge puts a mark on the score sheet at the exercises reviewed with the captain. The video can be reviewed in slow motion and paused. If the infrastructure for video review fails, an own video taken at the same angle as the judge can be used. If there is no video at all, the judge is right and the penalty stays on the sheet.","Only the judge on review duty can give instructions to the video technician. The captain has at most 30 minutes after leaving the video review, receiving and signing her sheets, to correct a sum error. After that they are not corrected. The judge must give the captain a photocopy of each score sheet (or the captain can take a photo if there is no copier).","Neither judges nor captains (nor instructors) can enter the video review with mobile phones or electronic devices at official events called by the FMCH. The score delivery is recorded on video with a camera with sound.","For State and National Championships, the judge must send a photograph of her sheets with the marked penalties to each captain before the video review. At events with charreada the team gets about 10 minutes to review the sheets. At escaramuza fairs all the sheets are sent to the teams and then the video review begins.","The judge can mark exercises 'En revision' (under review) when there is reasonable doubt about the penalty. Each team can have at most two exercises marked for review per routine, with at most 4 minutes (2 minutes if only one exercise is marked). These are reviewed first in the video review, with the captain present, outside the official 15 minutes. When finished, the penalty is decided and the 15-minute general review starts. The marked exercises are written on the score sheet with the remarks, and the captain signs as proof of being present (it does not mean she agrees). This is only for serious penalties such as a collision, a refusal or a disqualification for any reason. Every review is video recorded from when the captain enters the review area to the closing of the sheets and the signing of the score.","National Championship (Libre) video review: the whole National Championship is judged by official judges. The review starts when the captain arrives. If any judge has marked a serious infraction (collision, refusal, disqualification for any reason, not meeting the degrees of the turns, direction changes, bonus points, not meeting the Compulsory Routine) it is checked first, before the 15 minutes. If it matches, all judges must mark it. Then the 15 minutes run.","At the National Championship (Libre) the highest and lowest score are discarded, after proving the penalties in the video. A judge who is external to the 5 judges who score explains the three sheets of the judges that go into the average, shows the captain the infractions and proves each on the videos. If the infraction does not match or cannot be shown even using all video angles, she can remove it. The booth judge (if there is one) helps the responsible judge to confirm the marked penalty. The summary is always made on the sheet used to score the puntas. The delivery of scores to the press and video is rotated by the five judges per day.","Booth judge (if there is one) covers: collision, refusal, disqualification of exercises for any reason, not meeting the marked degrees of turns, anything about direction changes, bonus points and not meeting the Compulsory Routine."]},{"Key":"complaints","Title":"Refutation (Appeal) and Complaints","Items":["If the captain disagrees with a penalty marked by the judges, she can use the Single Refutation Format (Formato Unico de Refutacion), asked from the judges. It must say clearly which sheet is refuted, which exercise, which penalty and the reason.","A refutation is not allowed for the time of the video review or for a whole sheet in general. Each team can refute only 4 penalties in total of its competition.","The letter is given to the responsible judge on the same day of the competition in State Competitions, and 20 minutes after signing the score sheet in the National Championship, to be valid.","The request is evaluated by the Technical Council named by the National Coordination of Escaramuzas. The answer is given in 10 working days for State Competitions, and at the end of the competition for the National Championship. The answer letter is signed by a representative of the Technical Council and the National Coordinator of Escaramuzas.","The result is made known to the judge who penalised the event and to the delegate and the president of the Union of Associations of the state. Refutations and their resolutions are made public with the grounds, to keep transparency and fairness.","The captain keeps the refutation sheet to use in any clarification before the FMCH.","In states where a team refutes, the prize giving cannot be held until the refutation is resolved, unless that score does not change the order of the first 3 places.","If the penalty being refuted was in favour of the team, and the same penalty is marked on the same rider on the other sheets, those points are also given back.","If the refutation is not accepted, the team gets -5 for each reviewed penalty on its final score.","Captains must also accept the judges' decision. A complaint about the judging goes in the part of the sheet meant for it, and complaints about other teams are not allowed there."]},{"Key":"reporting","Title":"Reporting and Results","Items":["The score is official when the captain and the jury sign the score sheet. Without the captain's signature the score is not official.","Each judge hands the captain the score in a private place; the delivery is recorded with video and sound and the recording is given to the responsible judge the same day.","The responsible judge fills the report format, attaches it to the scored sheets and sends them to the FMCH in at most five working days after the event.","Judges send the Final Official Scores report at once by electronic means to the National Coordinator, the Secretariat of Sport and the FMCH Press and Dissemination Secretariat.","Decimals: when a final result has decimals they are not rounded. The score of the escaramuza keeps its decimals.","Rankings: the score used for the General Classification Table and for the State Champion of each state is described under Rounds and Advancement."]},{"Key":"points","Title":"Points and Ties","Items":["Tie-breaks in order: (1) the team with fewer total infractions on its score sheet wins; (2) the team with more 28-point turns with 10 bonus points; (3) the team whose compuesto exercise has the 8 turns with more degrees together; (4) the team with fewer infractions in turn defects; (5) the team with the higher sum of the total of puntas; (6) the team with more metres of the drag marks (arrastre) of both puntas; (7) at last, the immediately earlier OFFICIAL score of the teams involved.","Minimum score of 290 points for a Libre State Champion to get a direct pass to the Congress and National Championship, and for other teams to use their State score for the general classification.","Base value of a Libre or Juvenil routine before puntas and additional points: at most 304. Additional points: at most 52.","Details of the penalties, additional points and sums are listed in the Escaramuza competition rules."]},{"Key":"prizes","Title":"Prizes and Recognition","Items":["The State Champion of each state gets a plaque of State Champion and the prize, if there is one. A team that has a pass to the National can compete only for the prize or the plaque.","The regulation does not print prize amounts or trophies."]},{"Key":"payments","Title":"Payments","Items":["Riders and teams pay their affiliation (federation) fees and give copies of the receipt or deposit with their papers. The regulation does not print the amounts.","The delegate gives each captain on time a list of all expenses of every official event and then proves them to the teams or their representatives, with a copy to the FMCH and the National Coordinator of Escaramuzas.","Hosts pay the judges' fees, hotel, food and transport, from when they leave home to their return, including extra days, and the courier fee for sending the official papers to the FMCH. The delegate makes sure this is done.","Judges pay the costs of their own training. The costs of going out to judge officially are paid by the host or the FMCH under the current tariff."]},{"Key":"venue","Title":"Arena","Items":["Competitions are held in a charro arena (lienzo charro) with the size and marks described in the General Rules. The host association provides the arena, medical service (ambulance) from the practice, official male judges, announcers, and sound with two wireless microphones.","Teams must keep the practice times given to each team, even if it is without horses. The host arena cannot be used to practice after the event starts, except in the team's own participation, or the team is penalised with -6 points. A team that decides not to use its practice time is not penalised. In practice, all eight riders must wear their hats, or they cannot use their official practice time.","Once the official training times have started, teams cannot practice in the host arena at any other time even with the host's permission, except in state championships. In states where there is a host escaramuza, it can keep practising once the state championship of the charros or the official recognitions of the escaramuzas have started, in coordination with the association.","Teams can be given extra time on the competition day to warm up the puntas with recognition of the rectangle, in coordination with the delegate and the competition times."]},{"Key":"other","Title":"Attire, Saddle and Tack","Items":["A rider must appear properly: dressed as Adelita or charra with her horse and tack complete, only as the regulation says, by the category of dress worn at the tack check. A missing element, a forbidden element, or objects or makes that alter charro traditions disqualify the team. All parts of the attire must be worn alike by the members of the same escaramuza.","Types of dress in competition: the Adelita dress, or the charra suit in the faena, media gala or gala categories. The gran gala suit, the etiqueta suit and the China Poblana suit are never used in escaramuza competitions (they are for presentation contests, social, cultural and official events; the China Poblana suit is also used in parades and escort).","Adelita dress fabric: mostly cotton (poplin, manta, linen, embroidered strip, tergal or cotton with polyester). Transparent, satin, organza, fluorescent, gold, silver, suede, leather, velvet or cashmere fabrics are forbidden.","Adelita dress make: one piece or two pieces (skirt and blouse), of the same design among the eight riders. The waist is cut at the waist, not the hip. Colours can differ in pairs, quartets or individually (eight different colours). A sewn-on faldon trim is allowed.","Blouse: plain, with bib, with ruffle, or bib with trims and/or embroidery, following tradition. Blouses buttoned at the front and/or of corset type are forbidden.","Skirt ruffle (olan): at least one fabric ruffle, pleated, sewn separately at the bottom of the dress or skirt, at least 10 cm wide and/or in proportion to the rider's height. It cannot be longer than the base of the skirt. Thick tulle or wire-type under the last ruffle is allowed if it does not go beyond it and in permitted colours. Irregular wave ends are allowed if they are part of the fabric finish.","Ruffles are allowed from halfway down the leg (waist to knee) downward, as many as you choose, not higher. 'Duck tails' are allowed at the back or on one side if they do not change the traditional style. Multiple ruffles made one after another from the waist are forbidden.","Dress trims must have an opaque base: fabric (including sublimated fabric), embroidered strip, cotton or polyester lace, ribbons, crochet, cotton herringbone, painted, discreet embroidery, sewn flowers, small raised flowers, handmade appliques, woven pieces, rebozo fabric or fringe without the tips. 3D trims are allowed up to 20 x 10 cm and not as the main motif of the dress; the base must be sewn to the fabric. Tulle, guipure, organza, lace or embroidered gauze may be opaque or satin if made so by nature. Painted or woven drawings that vary are not a difference.","Forbidden dress trims: pearls, tubular beads, glass beads, sequins, studs, wood, stone or suede applications; fluorescent, silver, gold, metallic, neon or glaring colours; feathers; metal buttons (silver, gold, alpaca or similar); rebozo fringe tips, guipure strand ends or small ball ends. The dress cannot be fully decorated with raised applications.","Collar: attached, complete and high; can be crossed; made of the same fabric as the dress and/or a mix with its trims. Not detached, open or very low.","Sleeves: long, three-quarter or short, in the old style. The bellows or pleats of the sleeve must be noticeable. A short sleeve is halfway between shoulder and elbow (as printed). Crossed sleeves must cross enough not to show the arm. Armpits must never show. A back zip or buttons are optional.","Buttons: only as trim on the blouse and not the main motif. Pearly buttons are allowed. Metal, gold, silver or alpaca buttons are not.","Sash (banda): a complete sash at the waist, or fixed from the sides to the back, with a butterfly bow and/or tails at least 40 cm long from the made bow, is mandatory whether or not the rebozo is worn. A belt-type sash with a pre-sewn butterfly bow is allowed. It can be made of rebozo fabric, including its fringe.","Dress length: halfway down the Jalisco boot, with the crinoline on.","Hair bow (mono): of fabric, rebozo (with fringe), satin ribbon, matt ribbon, opaque organza ribbon or cotton lace matching the dress or the rebozo, not oversized. Worn with all the hair gathered at the nape and fixed to the hair. It cannot be stuck to the hat, dress or to any object to simulate it (for example a headband, elastic or thread). Flowers are allowed if sewn on the bow base. The eight bows of a team must be alike. Shiny organza, sequins, feathers, tubular beads, glass beads, paper, stones, fluorescent, gold, silver or iridescent decorations are forbidden and disqualify. Design is free (butterfly, segments and so on).","Crinoline: not a slip. It fluffs the dress, so it has one or more pleated ruffles. Natural fibres (cotton, poplin, cabeza de indio, manta or embroidered strip) in white, ecru or beige. It can have lace or point trims in the same colours and ribbon in a ribbon slot matching the dress. A side opening is allowed. The waistband has a button, elastic or ties. It need not be the same for the eight riders. Tulle is not allowed. It must not show under the dress. It is mandatory and must be starched, or the rider gets -2.","Calzoneras (long bloomers): of cotton, poplin, cabeza de indio or manta in white, ecru or beige. They need not be the same for the eight riders. With elastic waist, no zip or pockets, long to the ankle, worn inside the Jalisco boot. They can have trims (lace, embroidered strip, ribbon slot or ribbon, matching the dress). They cannot be made entirely of embroidered strip. White or beige leggings or lycra can be worn under them. Use is mandatory and must be long enough so that the boot covers them. If they come out of the boot and show the leg, the team gets -2 in 'Infractions in Presentation'.","Rebozo: a handmade artisan garment. It cannot be replaced by charro sashes, shawls, or fringed cloth that imitates a rebozo. Use is mandatory. Worn knotted at the waist (not the hip) with the knot on the left, leaving the dress sash below. It must match or contrast with the dress and/or trims in colour and tone. It can vary in tone but not in colour, and in fringe (traditional or pointed end). Different colours may be used in pairs, quartets or individually in the same team. Forbidden: fluorescent colours, applied, painted or embroidered decorations, gold or silver thread, charro sashes or any substitute, shawls, rebozos with small balls on the fringe ends, braided or woven beards. When not riding it can be worn over one or both shoulders with Porfirian boots.","Boots (Adelita): traditional Jalisco style, plain, thin sole, round toe, spur heel, made of leather, nubuck or suede. The shaft reaches 3/4 of the calf. The eight riders wear the same material, with a lace at mid-boot and a horseshoe on the outside of each boot. The top can have a cord or greca. Initials or the brand are allowed and need not be alike. Colours must be serious and the same among the eight (the tone can differ, not the colour; a different colour is penalised as a different attire, -2). Allowed Jalisco boot colours: beige, bone, ivory, honey, ochre, brown, maroon, wine, grey, dark green, navy blue and old gold. No zippers or elastic, no studs, no applied metal or plastic accessories.","Porfirian boots (with buttons or laces) are used when not riding. Allowed colours are the same as the Jalisco boot plus white and black. With the Porfirian boot the calzonera is short and the rebozo is worn over the shoulder or crossed.","Hat (Adelita): palm or soyate (the most traditional with the Adelita dress); felt, fur or wool are allowed. Ironing style is free. The eight riders wear the same in material, colour and ironing; tone can vary from use, sun fading, dyeing or the different craftsmen, not colour. The chin strap is leather, horsehair or cotton and when riding it is recommended under the chin.","Toquilla (hat band): simple rope or traditional braided rope (calabrote). The cut-out work in suede, painted vaqueta (in allowed colours), pyro-engraved, chiseled, chumeteado and piteado can be combined with each other, and only these. Sequins, tubular beads or glass beads on any of these work disqualify. Embroidered toquillas can use all colours except neon, fluorescent, iridescent or glaring colours. The cords marking an embroidered toquilla can be gold, silver or any colour. French galloon, embroidery in gold or silver thread, glass beads, tubular beads and sequins are allowed on embroidered toquillas.","Hat edge trim (ribete or carpetas) must equal the toquilla in material and design. Embroidery and cut-out drawings can vary keeping the original design. Tones can vary by use, sun fading, dyeing or different craftsmen. The toquilla and the ribete in gold and silver embroidery can be made on the hat body (not a craft applied instead of the embroidery). Embroidery on cloth must not be shiny or metallic.","Forbidden on the hat: toquillas that are all metal; applications of metal, bone, plastic, studs, buttons and so on other than the chapetas; any hanging object, even if made of the same hat work; ribbon-embroidered (craft) hats.","Chapetas (hat side pieces): each rider wears one identical pair; the pairs need not be exactly the same among the eight, only the same concept. On palm or soyate hats they are not mandatory; if used, only of leather, horn, bone or suede. On fur, felt or wool hats they must be metal (silver, alpaca, copper, brass, gold) or leather (leather can be sewn into the hat body if it matches the toquilla and/or ribete). If metal chapetas are used they are the only metal allowed on the hat. Embroidered chapetas on the hat body in the allowed colours are accepted if they match the toquilla or ribete.","Chapetas: old coins (more than 100 years) can be used inserted with a screw or staple. Do not mix categories. Applied metal chapetas must be inserted with a screw or staple on the sides of the crown, crafted by hand. A leather base under metal chapetas is allowed without protruding. Glued chapetas are disqualifying. Chapetas are charro items made by craftsmen: nothing may replace them (earrings, thongs or other objects). A missing chapeta costs -6 points per rider.","Earrings: any metal with artisan work; can have marquesitas, pearl, amber, any precious or semiprecious stone and/or zirconia, small or discreet, not the main concept of the earring. Each rider wears one identical pair. They need not be the same among the eight. If the rider has no ear piercings, not wearing them is not a reason to disqualify. If she has piercings and does not wear them, she is disqualified.","Charra suit colours (faena, media gala, gala) for boots, suedes and cashmeres, tie, hair bow and fajilla: beige, honey, ochre, brown, wine, old gold, grey, maroon, dark green, navy blue, dry orange and red (the last two only in bow, tie and fajilla). Bone may be used in suede.","Fajilla, tie and hair bow (charra suit): the tie and the bow hang and are both butterfly-shaped. The bow is worn with all the hair gathered at the nape. They can be of any fabric in permitted colours, satin or rebozo. The three pieces must be of the same fabric and equal among the 8 riders. Fringes and rebozo bows, and gold and silver thread embroidery are allowed. Flowers embroidered with glass beads, sequins, tubular beads, gold and silver threads are allowed on the tie and the bow. The three pieces cannot be made fully of suede, ante or leather, nor of gold fabrics, gauze, silver, fluorescent, pastel, pink, violet or iridescent fabrics.","The fajilla is worn in addition to the belt, going around the waist, and cannot come out under the belt. Its ends hang no more than a quarter (20 cm). The fringe at the ends cannot be left out. They can hang on both sides of the hip or only on the left.","Trousers (charra suit): the same cashmere as the skirt or a similar fabric with high cotton content; same colour as the skirt or the suit or a combination, only in the dark colours allowed for suits. Long to the ankle and worn inside the boot. Made with waistband, zip or elastic, without pockets. Forbidden fabrics (transparent, lining, satin, shiny, fluorescent, gold, silver, white, beige or manta) disqualify. Leggings or lycra of the same colour can be worn underneath.","Boots and earrings of the charra suit follow the same rules as for the Adelita dress, in the permitted colours. Hat with the faena suit: palm or soyate only with the charra faena suit; felt, fur or wool are allowed. The eight must wear the same in material, colour and ironing. Toquilla and chapetas follow the rules above.","Charra faena suit: worn with shirt and skirt, or jacket, shirt (attached turned civil-type collar), skirt, and an optional vest. Jacket and skirt colours: beige, honey, ochre, brown, wine, old gold, grey, maroon, dry green, navy blue; bone for suede.","Faena shirt: cotton of pachuqueña type or 'mao' collar in white, bone, beige, pastel colours, blue, grey, brown, wine, dry green, or discreet prints in permitted colours, with stripes or checks on white base. It can be lightly embroidered with matt and/or satin threads, only in pastels, beige, bone, ivory, honey, ochre, brown, red, old gold, wine, maroon, grey, dry green, navy blue and dry orange. Silver, gold, fluorescent, neon, purple, fuchsia, bright orange, bright yellow, shiny and glaring colours disqualify. If worn with a turned civil or military collar, a jacket must be worn. Pearl, silver, gold, painted or alpaca buttons are not allowed. Embroidery must be traditional charro (grecas, flowers) with no raised appliqued pieces (hook, crochet) that are not embroidered directly on the fabric.","Faena shirt can also be of cashmere of the same fabric as the skirt, with attached turned civil or military collar, with or without suede trim. Buttons of suede or tarugos of suede, horn or bone, three on each sleeve. Both shirts can have only bias trim matching or contrasting with the skirt, or cotton fabric trims. Rebozo is not allowed.","Faena jacket: plain, of cashmere, jerga or suede, with a suede alamar at the front and three suede, horn or bone tarugos on each sleeve. It can have suede or leather trims and an optional vest. No metal buttons. Worn with a shirt in white, bone, pastel, blue, grey, brown, wine, dry green, or discreet prints in permitted colours with stripes or checks on white base. The collar is attached and turned. When this attire is worn the jacket must never be taken off.","Faena skirt: A-line and long to the boot heel. Of cashmere or jerga. It can have suede or leather trim, be plain or with aleton; can have mecapal; can have a pleat on the left or on both sides with suede or leather trim and suede buttons or tarugos of horn or bone. The pleat can open from where the leg starts. Thread embroidery is not allowed. Chazarilla is allowed plain, cut-out, with suede or vaqueta trim, with suede buttons or tarugos of horn or bone, always buttoned fully, with 3 buttons on each sleeve, bow tie and white pachuqueña shirt under it. Buttons must not be metal.","Belt (faena): traditional charro style, of leather, nubuck or suede, matching the boots in material and colour. Buckles can be oval, rectangular, square or round, of the same material as the belt (not metal), and can have an iron of the same material, for example chiselled or pyro-engraved.","Charra media gala suit: with jacket, skirt, shirt with attached turned civil-type collar and optional vest. Only these colours for jacket and skirt: beige, bone, honey, ochre, brown, wine, old gold, grey, maroon, dry green, navy blue; for embroidery with matt threads also ivory, dry orange and red. Pastel, pink, fuchsia, gold, silver, violet, purple, bright yellow, neon, iridescent, fluorescent and bright or glaring orange are not allowed. Embroideries must be traditional charro, with no raised applied pieces.","Media gala jacket: of jerga, cashmere, suede or a combination, embroidered in pita, pyro-engraved, chumeteado, entresacados, pespunteado or chiselled, or with matt threads of the serious permitted colours. Mandatory: at least one silver or alpaca brooch on the chest and three cufflinks (mancuernas) of the same material aligned on each sleeve. Vest optional, in the same colour or in combination with the suede suit, with silver, alpaca or gold buttons. It can have mecapal. A chazarilla with buttons of the same characteristics is allowed.","Media gala skirt: A-line to the heels; of jerga, cashmere, suede or a mix, with the same embroidery options. Three silver or alpaca cufflinks aligned on both sides at hip height. A pleat on the left or both sides with trim matching the work of the suit; buttons on the aleton of the same material as the button set or suede tarugos; can have mecapal. Studs, buttons and so on that are not part of the mandatory button set disqualify. Shirt: turned or civil collar, opaque fabrics, only in white, beige, bone, pastel tones, blue, grey, dry green, brown or wine; no metal buttons. Belt: traditional charro style, of leather, nubuck or suede matching the boots; no extra work except chumeteado, piteado, pespunteado or chiselled; buckles oval, rectangular, square or round of the same material as the buttons.","Charra gala suit: jacket, shirt, skirt and optional vest, in the same colours as the media gala suit. Jacket: jerga, cashmere or suede or a mix, with pita chumeteado, entresacados, pespunteado or chiselled work, or matt thread embroidery in permitted colours; at least one gold, silver or alpaca brooch on the chest and three matching cufflinks on each sleeve; vest optional with buttons of the same material and concept as the cufflinks.","Gala skirt: A-line to the heels; jerga, cashmere, suede or a mix. It must have the complete gold, silver or alpaca button set aligned on the left side of the skirt, and only three cufflinks of the same material and concept aligned on the right side at hip height (for riding). Studs or buttons that are not part of the mandatory button set disqualify. Shirt: opaque fabrics only, in white, bone or beige, with attached turned civil-type collar. Belt: traditional charro style of leather, nubuck or suede matching the boots; can be chumeteado, piteado, pespunteado or chiselled when it matches the saddle work; buckles of the same material as the button set; decorations of typical charro style, can be personalised.","Gran gala suit (never in escaramuza competitions): for presentation contests and social, cultural and official events. Jacket, skirt and optional vest of cloth, cashmere or suede, opaque and in serious colours except black, white or ivory; with at least one gold, silver or alpaca brooch and three cufflinks on each sleeve; embroidered in gold and/or silver thread on sleeves and lapel; skirt with button set along both sides or horseshoe embroidery in gold or silver at front and back; white shirt with attached turned civil collar; tie, bow and fajilla of the same fabric in permitted colours; hat of fur or felt matching the suit; toquilla and ribete with galloon or embroidery in gold or silver; chapetas in gold, silver or alpaca; traditional Mexican handmade gold, silver or alpaca earrings; optional revolver with dragona; hardware (herraje) in silver or richly inlaid on iron is mandatory in this category.","Etiqueta suit (never in escaramuza competitions): not worn by day or on horseback; only for special cases such as weddings or funerals (with the button set covered by a veil out of respect). Jacket, skirt and vest matching, only of cloth or cashmere in black, white or ivory. Can be decorated with buttons (gold, silver or both), with embroidery in gold or silver thread, or both. White shirt with attached turned civil collar. Fajilla, tie and hair bow in white and bone. Belt of patent leather or suede in black, white or bone, no carrilleras. Boots Jalisco style of patent leather or suede in black, white or bone, no zippers. Felt hat in black, grey, bone or white. Chapetas gold or silver. Earrings traditional, Mexican, handmade, gold or silver. Revolver optional.","China Poblana suit (never in escaramuza competitions): for presentation contests, social, cultural and official events, parades and escort. White blouse with short sleeves and discreet neckline, embroidered with silk threads and/or coloured glass beads; tricolour satin castor or zagalejo (red, white, green) embroidered with sequins, tubular beads and glass beads, can have an eagle at the front and the Aztec calendar at the back; red or green silk slippers; tricolour, green or red rebozo worn in a cross; white petticoat with lace points showing at most 10 cm; gold, coral or papelillo necklaces, earrings or large hoops, and bracelets; hair in two long braids with ribbons and butterfly bows, or a bun; felt hat with galloons or fine gold and silver embroidery.","Riders must ride in albarda, with stirrups on both sides and side-saddle (a mujeriegas). Horse tack: the albarda must keep its traditional style. Three categories: plain (lisa), gala without hardware, and gala with hardware.","Albarda leather colours: natural ranges, honey, tannin, rust, wine, brown, black and white (black and white only in gala without hardware and gala with hardware). Colours can be combined in one albarda. In the plain category the saddles can vary in colour between each other if they keep the plain characteristics. In gala categories the tone can vary but not the colour. Tree parts may be dyed the same tone as the seat or the vaqueta; vaqueta can be behind the teja. A change of tone from wear is not a tack difference.","Painting is only allowed on the crafted work specified, in white, black, brown, ochre, grey, red and wine. Do not paint headstalls, pecho-pretales, correones, stirrups or saddles to imitate the work of the category shown (with corrector, marker, shoe paint and so on). Square or rectangular stirrups, not Texan style. No applications of steel, metal, bone and so on that alter the saddle design; hardware must be limited to the pieces of the hardware set. The gargantón is allowed only in parades and presentation contests; using it in an escaramuza competition disqualifies the team. Applications that are not leather, hide or suede, unspecified drawings or landscapes disqualify in any saddle category.","Plain albarda (lisa): no crafted work laid on the leather. Cantinas are optional. Changing the traditional style disqualifies. With the traditional enreatado, chapetones are mandatory (bone, horn, suede or plain vaqueta, not metal; they need not be alike; round or flat), for safety. A missing one costs -2. Albardas with another type of enreatado (twisted or made of ixtle rope) need no chapetones and are not penalised without them. Rings are round and may be lined or not. Category rings are not allowed in plain albardas. The counter-whip (contra latigo) can be short or wrapped. Sheepskin protection, irons, initials or pyro-engraved legends are not penalised as a tack difference. Plain saddles match in material the headstall, correon and pecho-pretal if of vaqueta; guards allowed if the same colour as the vaqueta. A textile pecho-pretal must match the cinch in concept and colours. In escaramuza competitions the plain saddle can be used with the Adelita dress, the faena suit and the media gala suit.","Gala albarda without hardware: embroidered in pita, matt thread, pyro-engraved, chiseled, pespunteadas, braided, chumeteadas, cordoned, embroidered or painted with artisan drawings (flowers, vines or grecas) following tradition, with contrasting guards and turned leather. It can have one work or a combination. The eight saddles must be alike and the whole set must match in material and work, except the headstall, which can have only one of the concepts. Chapetones are mandatory with the traditional enreatado (not metal). Engraving and chiselling can vary if it is the same type of craft. At least three parts of the saddle must carry the work, and the bastos are one of the mandatory ones (horns, enreatados, guards, cantinas, arciones, stirrups and so on), except the counter-whips; if not, it is penalised as a different saddle. In escaramuza competitions it can be used with the Adelita dress, the faena, media gala and gala suits.","Gala albarda with hardware: the same crafted work and rules as the gala albarda without hardware, with hardware (silver, alpaca or steel) on the pieces of the set. The chapetones of the saddle, pecho-pretal and headstall of the 8 riders must be the same concept. The rings of the cinches must also match the hardware in concept and material, not in design, or -2. The drawing of the hardware on the saddle parts must be the same concept and craft among the 8 riders. Zirconia, pearls and so on disqualify. A textile pecho-pretal is allowed and must match in colours and concept with the other textile tack, and its centre ring must be of the same material, concept and work as the other hardware (not the same design) or -2. It can be used with the Adelita dress, faena, media gala and gala suits.","Tack (arreos) colours allowed: brown (cafe), ochre, navy blue, old gold, wine, grey, beige, red, maroon, ivory, shedron or brick (without orange, pink or yellow tones), bone, honey, black, white, dark green and flag green. Any other colour not specified disqualifies the team. Small artisan differences in textiles (width, drawing size, tone fading from use and material) are not penalised.","Tack must be worn complete and alike in colours among all riders of a team (for example, if the rein has white, red and black, so must the sarape, cinch, mantilla, bozalillo, cuarta and textile pecho-pretal). Grecas, stripes or names are not differences if all riders wear them alike. Sponsors or advertising are allowed only on the sarape up to 30 x 10 cm and on hand protectors up to 10 x 10 cm. Braiding, decorating, tied tails or tails gathered to the saddle, false pieces or any paint on the horse's body disqualify.","Bit (freno): charro style with charro barbadas, with or without barberillos if the bit style has them. False rein or any non-charro bit (articulated, cowboy or Zacatecan) disqualifies.","Vara (stick): carried in the hand, mandatory; at least 70 cm; not covered, painted or with maniota. A spare can be on the saddle; if the stick drops during the performance it is loss of tack.","Cuarta: hung on the cuartero of the albarda; the eight riders show it alike in concept and matching colours with the other tack, whatever the dress category. It is used if the vara is lost. A single escobetilla is allowed. It must measure at least 70 cm. It has three parts: maniota (raw leather, vaqueta or lechuguilla rope, 15 cm), cabo or regaton (animal legs, leather, lechuguilla rope or woollen textile; not painted; 30 cm) and pajuelas (raw leather, horsehair, vaqueta or lechuguilla rope; 30 cm).","Sarape, mantilla, cinch and textile pecho-pretal: they must match each other in colour(s) (optional to share the same design; if a mantilla is used it must match the sarape in colours and design). The design and colour mix must be made when it is woven, and be equal among the 8 riders. On cinch and pecho-pretal, small, charro, traditional toys, avocados, coscojos and escobetillas are allowed if both carry them alike.","Sarape: mandatory, one piece, with fringes, or it is not a sarape (not complete means disqualification). It can have embroidered initials and/or names in permitted colours. It is rolled and tied, held only by the sarapero thongs even if the saddle has straps. The fringes are of the same material as the loom (braided if wool). Fringes sewn or inserted at the edge of the sarape sit on the riding (left) side when rolled, or centred along the roll with some falling on both sides. No objects may replace the sarape or fringes; no glued, sewn or applied applications. It can be arranged on the teja in 'candy' style in two colours or crossed if the sarape is one piece. Two or more sewn strips simulating a sarape disqualify, as do applications of any kind, including saddle work.","Mantilla: optional. If one rider uses it, the other seven wear it alike and matching the sarape in colour(s) and design. No substitutes or applied decorations. The part in contact with the arcion may be reinforced with vaqueta in the permitted colours with no work. Size and fringes do not matter.","Cinch (cincho): textile or horsehair, fully woven. Can be lined inside with sheepskin, felt, sarape or neoprene, not lined entirely, differently among riders without penalty. Plain, or with grecas, diamonds, stripes or flowers. Rings round, lined or not without penalty in plain saddles; with category hardware they must have the same material, concept and work as the hardware, not the same design. All-leather or all-sheepskin cinches or other unspecified materials are not allowed.","Pecho-pretal: mandatory; a metal ring with three straps (to the right ring, to the left ring, and between the horse's front legs to the cinch). Textile matching the other textile tack in colours, or vaqueta or leather matching the saddle in material and work. It can be lined inside or not, but not entirely, and cannot be made fully of sheepskin.","Headstall (cabezada): two pillars, ear piece and handles; leather or vaqueta with the work of the saddle category. Ear piece on the garrocha side if a buttonhole; if buttons, the upper one at the side of the nape on the mane. Rivets are not chapetones in the faena category and galletas do not count in plain saddles.","Rein (rienda): length varies with the horse; textile, raw leather or horsehair; plain, striped, candy, comb and so on; the eight of a team must be alike and match in colours with the other tack. It can have woven buttons or avocados. It must have a knot or button at the centre, or -1 per rider without it. Escobetillas, avocados, toys and coscojos are allowed if small, charro and traditional.","Bozalillo: three parts (bozal, headstall and lead); optional; must match the reins in colour and concept; the eight alike; used complete or with only two of the three parts; small charro decorations as above, alike among the eight.","Carona or sweat pad: used under the mantilla or alone, in serious colours (the same as allowed for tack); shapes like duck tail, round or square bastos, neoprene with sarape, sheepskin inside and/or at the edges; it must not stick out more than 10 cm from the saddle; coverings of mantilla allowed; need not be alike. Bajador: optional, charro style; need not be alike.","Spur strap (correon de la espuela): shape can vary; must match the saddle work category and be alike among the eight. Spur: charro style, the shank straight and horizontal and centred in the box, not bent; same material and work among the eight; rowels need not be alike; drawings can differ. It is not an ornament: its correct use and position is mandatory without altering its traditional design; worn on the heel of the left boot. Spurs with inlaid stones or pearls are forbidden.","Extra accessories (bajador, bozales, talonera, bells, belly pieces, hand protectors and so on) are optional and need not be alike. Protective bandages for the horse's legs are allowed if needed. Nose bands must sit at the height of the cartilage; allowed materials are vaqueta (plain, not altering tradition), cotton, horsehair or ixtle; the pensador, frentero or diadema are strictly forbidden. Bandage and protector colours are the same as permitted for tack.","Infractions in presentation (attire and/or tack): -2 points for each different concept of attire or tack, marked by the judge in the correct box. Exceptions: bits, caronas or sweat pads, plain saddles and extra accessories. Differences are counted by concept, not by item (for example, three different hats are one concept, -2). Losing any tack during the routine: -2 points."]}],"Documents":[{"Title":"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Categoria Libre 2024-2028 (Spanish, PDF)","Url":"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/wp-content/uploads/REGLAMENTO-DE-ESCARAMUZAS-LIBRE-2024-2028-B.pdf"},{"Title":"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas, Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil y Charras Mayores 2024-2028 (Spanish, PDF)","Url":"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/wp-content/uploads/REGLAMENTO-PARA-ESCARAMUZAS-Y-DAMAS-CHARRAS-CAT.-DL-IAIBJUVCHM-2024-2028-B.pdf"},{"Title":"FMCH page: Reglamentos Oficiales para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras 2026","Url":"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/reglamentos/reglamento-oficial-para-escaramuzas-2026/"},{"Title":"FMCH score and sketch sheets for Escaramuzas, Libre and Mayor categories 2026","Url":"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/sector-femenil/formato-femenil/hoja-de-calificacion-para-escaramuzas-2026/"}]}`),o=[{ParticipantType:"Team",Source:"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Categoria Libre, 2024-2028 (edition February 2026), Art. 1, 9, 57",Sections:{About:null,TimeAndFormat:null,Rules:["Every entry is a team of eight women riders (binomios of rider and horse). There are no individual entries.","All eight riders must ride the compulsory routine together and do all the exercises.","A team with fewer or more than eight riders at the moment of competing is disqualified."],Allowed:null,NotAllowed:null,Judging:null,Points:null,Notes:null},Markdown:`## Rules
- Every entry is a team of eight women riders (binomios of rider and horse). There are no individual entries.
- All eight riders must ride the compulsory routine together and do all the exercises.
- A team with fewer or more than eight riders at the moment of competing is disqualified.`}],n=JSON.parse(`[{"AgeGroup":"Infantil","Source":"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil y Charras Mayores, 2024-2028, Art. 15-17, 31-32, 36, 38-40, 48, 52-55","Sections":{"About":["The template group Infantil covers three categories of the regulation: Dientes de Leche, Infantil A and Infantil B. The exact birth years for each category are set each sports year by the Secretariat of Sport and are not printed in the regulation.","Infantil teams take part only in State Stage 1 (compulsory with 8 riders) and the National Charro Championship for Children, Youth and Escaramuzas; State Stage 2 is optional."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Riders compete in only one of Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B or Juvenil in all official competitions of the year.","All competitors ride in albarda and side-saddle. Riders may not be tied to the saddle.","Instructors: they must be properly dressed (charra or Adelita for women, charro for men; a hat is required with the charro suit) during the punta and the routine, or the punta or the routine is disqualified. They cannot film their team inside the ring or lane. Only one instructor, the one named on the score sheet, can go with the captain to the score delivery and the video review.","Instructors are the only people authorised to give directions during the performance of their team as support to the girls.","Instructors and helpers during the presentation: Dientes de Leche, at most two instructors and 4 helpers who can move in the ring, lane and/or partidero. Infantil A: two instructors per team who can move in the ring, lane and/or partidero. Infantil B: two instructors per team who can help only from the lane.","Instructors may give instructions without penalty: in Dientes de Leche and Infantil A they can move and walk all over the ring and/or lane, but cannot touch the riders or horses (contact in the punta disqualifies the punta; in an exercise, it disqualifies that exercise). In Infantil B they can move and walk only in the lane, without entering the ring. Contact with riders or horses is not allowed.","In the parade, two instructors and their helpers, properly dressed, can enter: up to 4 helpers in Dientes de Leche, up to 2 in Infantil A and only the trainer in Infantil B.","Participation time (puntas included): Dientes de Leche 15 minutes; Infantil A 11 minutes; Infantil B 11 minutes. For these three the clock starts when the instructors enter the partidero.","Puntas: Dientes de Leche, 1 punta, no need to drag the horse (rayar) and the drag marks are not measured; it starts with 5 points (one drag in one time) as long as it is not disqualified; it is done at trot or gallop and no physical contact with rider or horse is allowed (it disqualifies the punta). Infantil A, 1 punta, no need to drag, marks not measured, starts with 5 points; trot is not penalised, only a walk loses the speed bonus. Infantil B, 1 punta, starts at zero; the rider is not required to release the horse at full speed, but if it trots at any time after 20 metres it gets trot (-1). In Dientes de Leche and Infantil A, a rider changing to a mount outside the 8 that compete is a disqualification.","A trainer or accompanying person who checks the punta measure in Dientes de Leche, Infantil A and Infantil B must be correctly dressed and wear a hat with the charro suit, or the team is disqualified. Instructors can be present in the ring only to verify the punta measure.","Speed is not penalised: Dientes de Leche never (even walking, with limits listed below); Infantil A never; Infantil B only if the horse drops to a walk (P) at any time of the cuaje. Trot (T) is penalised in Infantil A and Infantil B (-2) when one or several riders trot. Paro (P) is penalised in Infantil B (-2) when riders drop to a walk or stop.","Distances for exercises: Dientes de Leche: 16-point exercises 3 horses; groupings 1 horse; the other values 3 horses. Infantil A and Infantil B: 16-point exercises 3 horses; groupings 1 horse; combs, cavalry charge and fan 1.5 horses lateral between each rider; 20-point exercises 2 horses (in two times, crosses and ladders 3 horses between the times, coladeras 4 horses); 26 and 28-point exercises 2 horses. Coladera circle opening: Infantil B more than 2 horses is the defect 'abierto'.","Video review: Dientes de Leche has 10 minutes; Infantil A, Infantil B and Juvenil have 15; the captain and her instructor do the review together.","Total routine value (suma base): Dientes de Leche 118 points; Infantil A 256 points; Infantil B 296 points. A base above the limit disqualifies the routine."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":["In Infantil teams a penalty for walking in a turn (4 points) applies only in Infantil B; in Dientes de Leche, if more than one exercise is done at a walk (including entry and/or exit) the penalty is -4 on the final score, if only one exercise -2.","In Dientes de Leche, a helper who is a minor costs -4 points. Helpers must be adults (at least 4, not required to be federated, no ID needed but the judges can ask for proof of age). In Dientes de Leche pulling a rider with a halter or any part of the tack, or physical contact with riders or horses to do the exercises, costs -4 each time per rider (except when the rider is at risk: about to fall, cannot control the horse, or the horse runs wild). Contact in Dientes de Leche punta disqualifies the punta."],"Points":["Dientes de Leche: routine of 6 exercises worth 118 points; with the punta the maximum is 129. Needed concepts (one each): grouping, pass and/or overtaking (pase/rebase), flower, carousel (carrusel), abanico (26 points), crossing (cruce), ladder (escalera). Values: 2 exercises of 16, 3 of 20, and 1 of 26 (the abanico). Giros, coladeras, combinado, compuesto and libre concept are forbidden. The abanico only needs half a turn of the ring. Three bonus points go to the team that has more than 4 riders aged 5 and 6.","Infantil A: base 256; maximum with the 3 additional points of each of the two 28-point exercises and the perfect punta is 273. Concepts: flower, grouping, pass and/or overtaking, carousel, crossing, ladder, and abanico (26). Flowers, groupings, crossings and ladders can be repeated at most 4 times, except the abanico which is done once. Values: 3 of 16, 5 of 20, 2 of 26 (including the abanico) and 2 of 28 with bonus (turns, coladeras, combinados and compuestos are forbidden). Giros, coladeras, combinado, compuesto and libre are forbidden.","Infantil B: base 296. Concepts (at least one each): coladera, cruce, giro, escalera, abanico (26), combinado, libre. Giro at most twice; coladeras, crossings, ladders and flowers at most three times including the two combinado exercises; libre can repeat cruce, escalera or coladera as a fourth concept. Values: one of 16, three of 20, two of 26 (one is the abanico), four of 28 (including the libre) and one combinado (28 giro plus one exercise of 16, 20, 26 or 28). Combinado totals: 28+28=296, 28+26=294, 28+20=288, 28+16=284. Maximum additional points 32 (two 360-degree turns of 28 with 10 each and four 28-point movements with 3 each).","Puntas start with 5 points in Dientes de Leche and Infantil A, and with zero in Infantil B. Additional points are added and infractions subtracted."],"Notes":["Birth years for each Infantil category are set each year by the Secretariat of Sport; the regulation does not print them.","The table of puntas in the regulation says Infantil A and Infantil B must drag the horse and score the drag, while the category articles say Infantil A need not drag and marks are not measured; the category article is followed for Infantil A."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- The template group Infantil covers three categories of the regulation: Dientes de Leche, Infantil A and Infantil B. The exact birth years for each category are set each sports year by the Secretariat of Sport and are not printed in the regulation.\\n- Infantil teams take part only in State Stage 1 (compulsory with 8 riders) and the National Charro Championship for Children, Youth and Escaramuzas; State Stage 2 is optional.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Riders compete in only one of Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B or Juvenil in all official competitions of the year.\\n- All competitors ride in albarda and side-saddle. Riders may not be tied to the saddle.\\n- Instructors: they must be properly dressed (charra or Adelita for women, charro for men; a hat is required with the charro suit) during the punta and the routine, or the punta or the routine is disqualified. They cannot film their team inside the ring or lane. Only one instructor, the one named on the score sheet, can go with the captain to the score delivery and the video review.\\n- Instructors are the only people authorised to give directions during the performance of their team as support to the girls.\\n- Instructors and helpers during the presentation: Dientes de Leche, at most two instructors and 4 helpers who can move in the ring, lane and/or partidero. Infantil A: two instructors per team who can move in the ring, lane and/or partidero. Infantil B: two instructors per team who can help only from the lane.\\n- Instructors may give instructions without penalty: in Dientes de Leche and Infantil A they can move and walk all over the ring and/or lane, but cannot touch the riders or horses (contact in the punta disqualifies the punta; in an exercise, it disqualifies that exercise). In Infantil B they can move and walk only in the lane, without entering the ring. Contact with riders or horses is not allowed.\\n- In the parade, two instructors and their helpers, properly dressed, can enter: up to 4 helpers in Dientes de Leche, up to 2 in Infantil A and only the trainer in Infantil B.\\n- Participation time (puntas included): Dientes de Leche 15 minutes; Infantil A 11 minutes; Infantil B 11 minutes. For these three the clock starts when the instructors enter the partidero.\\n- Puntas: Dientes de Leche, 1 punta, no need to drag the horse (rayar) and the drag marks are not measured; it starts with 5 points (one drag in one time) as long as it is not disqualified; it is done at trot or gallop and no physical contact with rider or horse is allowed (it disqualifies the punta). Infantil A, 1 punta, no need to drag, marks not measured, starts with 5 points; trot is not penalised, only a walk loses the speed bonus. Infantil B, 1 punta, starts at zero; the rider is not required to release the horse at full speed, but if it trots at any time after 20 metres it gets trot (-1). In Dientes de Leche and Infantil A, a rider changing to a mount outside the 8 that compete is a disqualification.\\n- A trainer or accompanying person who checks the punta measure in Dientes de Leche, Infantil A and Infantil B must be correctly dressed and wear a hat with the charro suit, or the team is disqualified. Instructors can be present in the ring only to verify the punta measure.\\n- Speed is not penalised: Dientes de Leche never (even walking, with limits listed below); Infantil A never; Infantil B only if the horse drops to a walk (P) at any time of the cuaje. Trot (T) is penalised in Infantil A and Infantil B (-2) when one or several riders trot. Paro (P) is penalised in Infantil B (-2) when riders drop to a walk or stop.\\n- Distances for exercises: Dientes de Leche: 16-point exercises 3 horses; groupings 1 horse; the other values 3 horses. Infantil A and Infantil B: 16-point exercises 3 horses; groupings 1 horse; combs, cavalry charge and fan 1.5 horses lateral between each rider; 20-point exercises 2 horses (in two times, crosses and ladders 3 horses between the times, coladeras 4 horses); 26 and 28-point exercises 2 horses. Coladera circle opening: Infantil B more than 2 horses is the defect 'abierto'.\\n- Video review: Dientes de Leche has 10 minutes; Infantil A, Infantil B and Juvenil have 15; the captain and her instructor do the review together.\\n- Total routine value (suma base): Dientes de Leche 118 points; Infantil A 256 points; Infantil B 296 points. A base above the limit disqualifies the routine.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- In Infantil teams a penalty for walking in a turn (4 points) applies only in Infantil B; in Dientes de Leche, if more than one exercise is done at a walk (including entry and/or exit) the penalty is -4 on the final score, if only one exercise -2.\\n- In Dientes de Leche, a helper who is a minor costs -4 points. Helpers must be adults (at least 4, not required to be federated, no ID needed but the judges can ask for proof of age). In Dientes de Leche pulling a rider with a halter or any part of the tack, or physical contact with riders or horses to do the exercises, costs -4 each time per rider (except when the rider is at risk: about to fall, cannot control the horse, or the horse runs wild). Contact in Dientes de Leche punta disqualifies the punta.\\n\\n## Points\\n- Dientes de Leche: routine of 6 exercises worth 118 points; with the punta the maximum is 129. Needed concepts (one each): grouping, pass and/or overtaking (pase/rebase), flower, carousel (carrusel), abanico (26 points), crossing (cruce), ladder (escalera). Values: 2 exercises of 16, 3 of 20, and 1 of 26 (the abanico). Giros, coladeras, combinado, compuesto and libre concept are forbidden. The abanico only needs half a turn of the ring. Three bonus points go to the team that has more than 4 riders aged 5 and 6.\\n- Infantil A: base 256; maximum with the 3 additional points of each of the two 28-point exercises and the perfect punta is 273. Concepts: flower, grouping, pass and/or overtaking, carousel, crossing, ladder, and abanico (26). Flowers, groupings, crossings and ladders can be repeated at most 4 times, except the abanico which is done once. Values: 3 of 16, 5 of 20, 2 of 26 (including the abanico) and 2 of 28 with bonus (turns, coladeras, combinados and compuestos are forbidden). Giros, coladeras, combinado, compuesto and libre are forbidden.\\n- Infantil B: base 296. Concepts (at least one each): coladera, cruce, giro, escalera, abanico (26), combinado, libre. Giro at most twice; coladeras, crossings, ladders and flowers at most three times including the two combinado exercises; libre can repeat cruce, escalera or coladera as a fourth concept. Values: one of 16, three of 20, two of 26 (one is the abanico), four of 28 (including the libre) and one combinado (28 giro plus one exercise of 16, 20, 26 or 28). Combinado totals: 28+28=296, 28+26=294, 28+20=288, 28+16=284. Maximum additional points 32 (two 360-degree turns of 28 with 10 each and four 28-point movements with 3 each).\\n- Puntas start with 5 points in Dientes de Leche and Infantil A, and with zero in Infantil B. Additional points are added and infractions subtracted.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- Birth years for each Infantil category are set each year by the Secretariat of Sport; the regulation does not print them.\\n- The table of puntas in the regulation says Infantil A and Infantil B must drag the horse and score the drag, while the category articles say Infantil A need not drag and marks are not measured; the category article is followed for Infantil A."},{"AgeGroup":"Juvenil","Source":"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil y Charras Mayores, 2024-2028, Art. 15-18, 56","Sections":{"About":["Juvenil teams are riders born in the years set by the Secretariat of Sport for the sports year (not printed)."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["Juvenil teams must compete with 8 riders in State Stage 1 to go to the National Charro Championship for Children, Youth and Escaramuzas. Stage 2 is optional.","A Juvenil team that also wants to go to the Congress and National Charro Championship (Libre) must take part in State Stage 1 and 2, with the same duties and rights as Libre teams, and can become Libre State Champion. It takes part only once in the State Championships and its score counts for both categories.","Juvenil riders can reinforce only one Libre team.","Participation time (puntas included): 8 minutes.","Puntas: 2 puntas, starting from zero.","The instructor can enter the video review, respecting the judges and meeting the regulation. No support is allowed from the instructor during puntas and routine.","Video review: 15 minutes with the captain and her instructor."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":["Judged as the Libre category. The routine includes at least one exercise of each concept: coladera, cruce, giro, escalera, abanico (26), combinado, libre and compuesto. Values: one of 16, two of 20, two of 26 (one is the abanico), five of 28 (including one compuesto and one libre) and one combinado. Base maximum 304. Additional points at most 52."],"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Juvenil teams are riders born in the years set by the Secretariat of Sport for the sports year (not printed).\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Juvenil teams must compete with 8 riders in State Stage 1 to go to the National Charro Championship for Children, Youth and Escaramuzas. Stage 2 is optional.\\n- A Juvenil team that also wants to go to the Congress and National Charro Championship (Libre) must take part in State Stage 1 and 2, with the same duties and rights as Libre teams, and can become Libre State Champion. It takes part only once in the State Championships and its score counts for both categories.\\n- Juvenil riders can reinforce only one Libre team.\\n- Participation time (puntas included): 8 minutes.\\n- Puntas: 2 puntas, starting from zero.\\n- The instructor can enter the video review, respecting the judges and meeting the regulation. No support is allowed from the instructor during puntas and routine.\\n- Video review: 15 minutes with the captain and her instructor.\\n\\n## Points\\n- Judged as the Libre category. The routine includes at least one exercise of each concept: coladera, cruce, giro, escalera, abanico (26), combinado, libre and compuesto. Values: one of 16, two of 20, two of 26 (one is the abanico), five of 28 (including one compuesto and one libre) and one combinado. Base maximum 304. Additional points at most 52."},{"AgeGroup":"Libre","Source":"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Categoria Libre, 2024-2028 (edition February 2026), Art. 9, 13-21, 31-32, 36, 38-40, 53","Sections":{"About":["The Libre category is judged as the Libre regulation says; its State Champions go to the Congress and National Charro Championship."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["A Libre competition is a contest between three or more Libre teams of the same state.","Participation time (puntas included): at most 8 minutes with no tolerance.","Puntas: 2 puntas, starting from zero.","Both State Championships must be attended to get the higher score for the National pass."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":["Libre routine: 12 exercises. Concepts: coladera, cruce, giro, escalera, abanico (26), combinado, libre and compuesto. Base maximum 304 points; additional points at most 52. The minimum score for a State Champion pass is 290 points."],"Notes":null},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- The Libre category is judged as the Libre regulation says; its State Champions go to the Congress and National Charro Championship.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- A Libre competition is a contest between three or more Libre teams of the same state.\\n- Participation time (puntas included): at most 8 minutes with no tolerance.\\n- Puntas: 2 puntas, starting from zero.\\n- Both State Championships must be attended to get the higher score for the National pass.\\n\\n## Points\\n- Libre routine: 12 exercises. Concepts: coladera, cruce, giro, escalera, abanico (26), combinado, libre and compuesto. Base maximum 304 points; additional points at most 52. The minimum score for a State Champion pass is 290 points."},{"AgeGroup":"Mayor","Source":"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil y Charras Mayores, 2024-2028, Art. 15, 19, 31-32, 57","Sections":{"About":["Charras Mayores: riders 45 years old and older. Younger riders cannot take part even if they are adults. The team can have riders of the same or different states."],"TimeAndFormat":null,"Rules":["The category competes only in the National Charros Mayores Championship. It does not have to attend a state championship.","Age bonus points per rider added to the score: 45 to 49 years, 0 extra points; 50 to 54, 2; 55 to 59, 4; 60 to 64, 6; 65 or more, 8.","This is the only category where saddles and tack can be different between riders and are not checked.","Participation time (puntas included): 11 minutes.","Puntas: 2 puntas, starting from zero. An external horse for the punta is allowed. A change of the punta horse is authorised for the safety and performance of the team."],"Allowed":null,"NotAllowed":null,"Judging":null,"Points":["Routine total (base): 296 points. Concepts: coladera, cruce, giro, escalera, abanico (26), combinado, libre. Giro at most twice; coladeras, crosses, ladders and flowers at most three times including the two combinado exercises; libre can repeat cruce, escalera or coladera as a fourth concept. Values: one of 16, three of 20, two of 26 (one is the abanico), four of 28 (including the libre) and one combinado. Combinado totals: 28+28=296; 28+26=294; 28+20=288; 28+16=284. Additional points at most 32 (as Infantil B).","Speed is not penalised unless the team drops to a walk (P) during the cuaje. Trot is penalised (-2)."],"Notes":["The template starts this group at age 40; the regulation says 45 years completed and older."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- Charras Mayores: riders 45 years old and older. Younger riders cannot take part even if they are adults. The team can have riders of the same or different states.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- The category competes only in the National Charros Mayores Championship. It does not have to attend a state championship.\\n- Age bonus points per rider added to the score: 45 to 49 years, 0 extra points; 50 to 54, 2; 55 to 59, 4; 60 to 64, 6; 65 or more, 8.\\n- This is the only category where saddles and tack can be different between riders and are not checked.\\n- Participation time (puntas included): 11 minutes.\\n- Puntas: 2 puntas, starting from zero. An external horse for the punta is allowed. A change of the punta horse is authorised for the safety and performance of the team.\\n\\n## Points\\n- Routine total (base): 296 points. Concepts: coladera, cruce, giro, escalera, abanico (26), combinado, libre. Giro at most twice; coladeras, crosses, ladders and flowers at most three times including the two combinado exercises; libre can repeat cruce, escalera or coladera as a fourth concept. Values: one of 16, three of 20, two of 26 (one is the abanico), four of 28 (including the libre) and one combinado. Combinado totals: 28+28=296; 28+26=294; 28+20=288; 28+16=284. Additional points at most 32 (as Infantil B).\\n- Speed is not penalised unless the team drops to a walk (P) during the cuaje. Trot is penalised (-2).\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The template starts this group at age 40; the regulation says 45 years completed and older."}]`),i=JSON.parse(`[{"Title":"Escaramuza (Synchronized Team Riding)","Source":"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Categoria Libre, 2024-2028 (edition February 2026), Art. 31-56; FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil y Charras Mayores, 2024-2028, Art. 31-56","Sections":{"About":["A team of eight women riders on horseback shows 12 exercises (6 in Dientes de Leche) of the Compulsory Competition Routine at the gallop, plus puntas (stopping demonstrations), judged by official judges.","Competition stages: terrain recognition; tack check; parade; puntas; entry of the routine; compulsory competition routine; exit of the routine; video review.","An exercise is each one of the movements of the routine. Every exercise has three observation moments: entry (one and a half horses before the cuaje), cuaje (the exact moment of the crossing or turn, at the centre of the ring except 16 and 20-point exercises) and exit (one and a half horses after the cuaje)."],"TimeAndFormat":["Time of participation, puntas included: Libre 8 minutes with no tolerance; Dientes de Leche 15 minutes; Infantil A and B 11 minutes; Juvenil 8 minutes; Charras Mayores 11 minutes.","The time starts when the first rider enters the arena and ends when, after the routine, the eight riders stop completely anywhere in the ring and greet in charro style. Overrun costs -5 points for each minute or part of a minute.","The team has one minute to start its competition when the Sports Commission or the caporal says the terrain is ready, or -2. This is one minute for the puntas and one minute for the routine.","The timer stops only for measuring the puntas and while the rectangle is cleaned. The time of each punta is added to the time of the routine."],"Rules":["Terrain recognition: teams respect their practice times. All eight riders wear hats in practice. Teams cannot practice in the host arena at other times, or -6.","Tack check (before the parade): attire and tack are checked, the original FMCH cards are compared with the copies on the sketch sheet and the riders are called by name. Teams sharing horses are not penalised: both teams come to the judges for the check, one team on foot and the other mounted; one of the teams must parade complete.","Parade: the competing team parades with 6 riders at the start of the competition. The 2 riders who will ride the punta stay outside the lane of the ring. Not doing so is -6. If a rider or horse cannot parade because of an emergency just before the parade, the judge can allow an incomplete parade without penalty. Falls, hands up, refusals, collisions, trot or contact in the parade are never penalised.","Entry of the routine: it starts when the first rider enters the arena completely and ends when the team starts the first exercise at the gallop after a complete stop (if not, the team is disqualified). The judge gives the entry signal to the Sports Commission. The eight riders enter grouped and gallop from 20 metres of the arena (-2 if not). They can do a preparatory exercise during the entry before the complete stop. Penalties during the entry while the timer is stopped are not applied (distances, execution defects, not keeping the coladera circle, no entry or exit, not marking preparations, 2 times, change of direction, exercise disqualifications).","The Compulsory Competition Routine is the 12 exercises the team shows at the gallop (6 in Dientes de Leche). All eight riders must do all the exercises, showing clearly the entry, cuaje and exit of each one. An exercise done with fewer than 8 riders is disqualified (not all 8 are in the cuaje).","As long as it meets the concepts and the values, the team can design the routine freely. It is drawn on both sheets, showing the place of each exercise in the ring.","If the team does more than 12 exercises, the extra ones are marked as preparatory on the score sheet and drawn on the sketch sheet, and are judged as preparations with the penalties that happen in them. Preparations with more than 6 riders together before a centre exercise or an exercise showing a value, if not marked as 'preparacion', cost -6 each omitted. This does not apply to the final flourish of the routine.","Music of the routine: traditional Mexican music played by mariachi, regional or banda. Rock, pasodoble or any modernist or foreign trend is forbidden, even with mariachi or banda, as is a personalised introduction of the team and music against the language and values of the charro sport. Not meeting this: -6 points.","Exit of the routine: it ends when, after the last exercise, the eight riders stop completely and greet in charro style. Everything until then is judged. Leaving without greeting and/or stopping costs -5 points and the timer runs until they leave the arena. The whole team must leave at the gallop to the end of the partidero, or -2.","The greeting is in charro style: touch the hat brim with the back of the right hand, facing the public, after a complete stop.","Libre and Juvenil routine (12 exercises) needs at least one exercise of each compulsory concept: coladera, cruce, giro, escalera, abanico (26 points), combinado, libre and compuesto. Giros, coladeras, crosses, ladders and flowers can be repeated at most three times, counting the two exercises of the combinado and the compuesto. In the libre exercise a cruce, escalera or coladera can be repeated as a fourth concept, marked as 'libre' on the score sheet.","Values: one exercise of 16 points; two of 20; two of 26 (one must be the abanico); five of 28 (including one compuesto and one libre); and one combinado (an independent compulsory concept of two exercises: a 28-point giro and a complementary exercise of any value 16, 20, 26 or 28, which must be made only of the compulsory concepts of the routine).","The maximum base value of a Libre routine, before puntas and additional points, is 304. The combinado decides the base total: 28+28=304, 28+26=302, 28+20=296, 28+16=292. A base above 304 disqualifies the team (not presenting the compulsory routine). The maximum of additional points is 52: two individual 360-degree giros of 28 points, a compuesto of 28 points with 2 giros of 360 degrees, and four 28-point movements (ladders, coladeras, crosses or libre) with 3 additional points each.","The concept of an exercise must match its design or the exercise is disqualified. The drawings are general designs to guide the execution and how it can be seen from outside.","The initial value of the exercises is checked on the sketch sheets and confirmed at the execution. Designs must match on both sheets. If the design is the same as the correct sheet (either the sketch sheet or the score sheet) the exercise is not disqualified. If it matches neither, the exercise is disqualified when it happens.","All exercises must be done by the eight riders, show entry, cuaje and exit clearly, and be simultaneous and/or with intersection. A direction change is allowed in all exercises except the combinado, the compuesto and coladeras (except the 26-point coladera), during the preparation of the exercise, keeping the same starting path during the entry of one and a half horses, the cuaje and the one and a half horse exit. The direction change counts from when the horse has fully changed its path with hands and feet. The moment and path of the change must be marked with red ink on the sketch and score sheet, or -2.","In linear exercises, the riders must advance a horse and a half of linear path before reaching the nose-to-tail intersection of the exercise.","Two exercises of the same design only with inverted directions, or the same design in another place in the ring, count as equal. Exercises with a perpendicular or diagonal intersection at the cuaje count as different.","Maximum distances: 16-point exercises 3 horses (groupings: 1 horse between riders); 20-point exercises 2 horses (in two times, crosses and ladders 3 horses between the times, coladeras 4 horses); 26 and 28-point exercises 1.5 horses between one mount and another whatever the design; flowers, combs, abanico and cavalry charge, being groupings, 1 horse between riders; turns in pairs max half a horse lateral to the partner, and the gap must not open more than one horse lateral or the exercise is disqualified.","16-point exercises: a cross of two riders; turns of two by two riders; individual turns with max 3 horses between the eight; coladeras where riders cross one by one; flowers where two or three riders change direction at once; overtakes (rebases) or passes (pases) of 8 riders; groupings of four or eight; ladders where one rider crosses at a time.","20-point exercises: a cross of four riders; turns of four riders; coladeras where two riders cross at the same time; flowers where at least four riders change direction at once; ladders where two, three or four riders cross at once. 20-point exercises must have the same design in both times, even in a different place, moment or direction, or at the same time. The team must mark '2T' when done in two times, or -2.","26-point exercises: a cross of eight riders at once with two directions; coladeras where four crossing riders cross the circle at once, only entering or only exiting; the abanico, the cavalry charge and the comb; ladders with two directions as most where four riders cross at once.","28-point exercises: crosses with three directions or more; turns of eight riders at once; a coladera where four riders enter and exit the circle at the same time; ladders with more than two directions where four riders cross at once; libre with eight riders crossing at once; compuesto where four riders turn in pairs and four individually at once.","In any exercise, if a rider is left at a distance of three horses from her team mates, or a gap opens between riders of more than three horses in the cuaje compared to the drawing, the exercise is disqualified (except 16-point exercises), and the penalties already made stay. If at the simultaneous intersection one or more riders have a separation of one horse, it is a timing defect (D). In any cuaje, an intersection at 1.5 horses or more at the edge of the exercise is disqualified because it leaves the rider outside the figure. The distance is judged progressively: distance, defect, disqualification.","Groupings (16): formations where the 8 riders gather whatever the design, in entry, cuaje and exit: horizontal, parallel, diagonal, octagonal, half abanico or circular; when in blocks of 4 the maximum gap between blocks is one horse. Circular exercises must make at least one full turn once formed. Half abanicos must make at least half a turn of the ring once formed, with at most one horse lateral between riders and at most 3 horses between abanicos. Single files of 4 or 8 riders (parallel or opposite) are not groupings: they are preparations with no value.","Pases and rebases (16): passes are movements of 8 riders passing in opposite directions (for example a tunnel, opposite passes). Rebase (overtake) are movements of 8 riders in the same direction from back to front. They must show the three times and be kept for three horses and are penalised like any exercise.","Flowers (16 and 20): movements of eight riders with a radial entry or exit, from the edge to the centre or the other way, with a change of direction. Supporting on the hindquarters while changing direction disqualifies the exercise. All eight riders must be in the design at the execution. The drawing is made with a continuous line showing the start and end of the radial and, on the sketch sheet only, the path from the end of the radial to the exit point of every rider is dotted in red ink. A flower of 16 points has two or three riders changing direction at the same time; a flower of 20 points has at least four.","Abanico (26): a diagonal exercise of eight riders where the leader goes along the ring wall. They must make one full turn of the ring once fully formed (8 riders). It keeps its traditional design. It is the only concept that has no entry and/or exit and only its execution is judged. Penalties for the same concept are not cumulative on the same rider (for example lateral distance, back, forward, trot, rub, defect).","Crosses (16, 20, 26 and 28): movements with one or more intersections, done perpendicular and/or diagonally by two or more riders crossing at once. Value depends on the distance margins, the number of riders and the number of directions: 16 points two riders, distance up to 3 horses; 20 points four riders, up to 2 horses; 26 points eight riders in only two directions, up to 1.5 horses in each gap; 28 points eight riders in three or more directions, up to 1.5 horses in each gap. If three or more riders are grouped at the exact moment of the cuaje it is not a cross; it is an unspecified exercise, without value and disqualified (not if the group forms at the entry or exit).","Ladders (16, 20, 26 and 28): exercises with a single column formed by a row going in one direction of two, three or four riders, which the other riders cross. All ladders must be drawn with the intersection at the same moment of the cuaje, in T or nose-to-tail. 16 points: cross one by one at the same time, with up to 3 horses between mounts, one column of two or four riders. 20 points: two or four riders cross at once, with at least 1.5 and at most 2 horses between the parallel, perpendicular or square columns (less than 1.5 horses disqualifies), one column of two, three or four riders, the same design. 26 points: all eight cross at once with up to 1.5 horses in two directions, in one column of four riders. 28 points: the columns of three or four riders go in one direction with up to 1.5 horses, crossed in two or more directions.","In a ladder with a row of 4 riders, only the 2nd and 3rd can go in the column with mounts nose to tail, not the 2nd to the 1st or the 4th to the 3rd. In 26 and 28-point ladders no two horses can go nose to tail in the column, or the exercise is disqualified. The team must cross through all the gaps of the column. The offsets of mounts that go in pairs in 20-point ladders must be at most up to the horn of the partner and equal between them, or the 'distance' penalty. 16 or 20-point ladders can go one after another, parallel in the same direction or in opposite directions, even in different places in the ring, if the two have the same design. When done in two times, the team must mark '2T'. Between the first and second time there must be a minimum distance of half a horse (the first time must fully cross, freeing the hindquarters, and advance half a horse before the second crosses) and at most 3 horses.","Coladeras (16, 20, 26 and 28): crosses where the main base is a circle. The circle must have four riders at the centre of the rectangle (except 16 and 20-point coladeras), while the other four cross it, entering and exiting, only entering or only exiting. Entry, cuaje and exit are linear in 16, 20 and 28-point coladeras. In 26-point coladeras the crossing riders can follow their circumference path and their entry or exit must be a straight line. Undoing the circle is done linearly. 16 points: cross the circle one by one with up to 3 horses between all mounts. 20 points: cross two by two with up to 1.5 horses distance in the circle. 26 points (only entry): four riders cross in each of the gaps of the circle when entering. 26 points (only exit): the circle is formed by the eight riders and four riders cross at the exit through each of the 4 gaps, in front of the next horse, and exit linearly. 26 points with two circles of 4 riders: all 8 follow the same path, and the cuaje is the 4 of the inner circle crossing the 4 of the outer circle through its 4 gaps. 28 points: four riders cross entering and exiting at the same time, linear entry, cuaje and exit. In 20, 26 and 28-point coladeras the maximum distance in the circle is 1.5 horses.","In all coladeras the circle must be formed during one and a half horses before the cuaje and kept one and a half horses after it, with the distances kept in the three times; it cannot close, open or undo (-2). A 26-point coladera whose circle makes more than one full turn before the cuaje is a preparatory movement; if not marked as such, -6. Coladeras of any value without the entry and/or exit: -2.","Giros (turns) (16, 20 and 28): exercises where the horses support their hindquarters to turn on their axis, keeping a radius equal to the length of the horse. The horse must keep the sequence and timing in the movement of its hands. It must not move back or sideways (displacement). 16 points: one by one, two by two, four by four or all at once with up to 3 horses distance. 20 points: four by four or all eight at once with up to 2 horses. 28 points: all eight at the same time, up to 1.5 horses.","Only turns of 180, 270 and/or 360 degrees are allowed. Riders can turn individually or in four pairs. Combining individual and pair turns is forbidden in the same exercise, except in the compuesto. If turns of different degrees are done, only two variants of the three are allowed (180 with 270, 270 with 360 or 180 with 360), with 4 riders each; all 8 start to turn at the same time and the exit depends on the degrees done. Turns right and left at once are allowed only if the team marks with red and green on both sheets who turns left and right, only four riders to each side, and all riders turn the same degrees.","In pair turns, riders can begin when they are parallel and their mounts are at least head to head of the partner's horse and at most when both are at tails. They end when they separate for the exit. The degrees start when the horses begin to turn. They do not need to be shoulder to shoulder. Distance in pair turns cannot be more than half a horse lateral with the partner at any time, or defect LATERAL (L); more than one horse lateral opens a disqualification. If the riders start to turn before being head to head with the partner, the team loses the bonus points for the degrees; if it looks like the turn is individual, not in pairs, it is disqualified.","Giros with direction change must change direction in the preparation, keeping the same starting path, linear or circular, during the entry, cuaje and exit. All 8 riders with a change of direction turn the same degrees and the same side. Entry and exit paths cannot be combined (circular in, linear out) except in the 270-degree turn. The change can be made by eight, or by one block of four together; never individually. If paths are combined by the eight, exactly 4 riders change linearly and 4 circularly (never 3 and 5, 6 and 2, and so on). In pair turns with a change of direction, there are four pairs, and the change and the exit must be linear. In 180 and 360-degree turns, the path of the change is done the same way in entry and exit. In 270 turns a circular entry can combine with a linear exit, or both linear. A circle formed to prepare the turn cannot advance more than half a turn after the change, or more than half a turn after the turn. A linear change cannot advance more than three horses after the change. Not meeting these costs -6 for the change of direction plus -2 for entry and/or -2 for exit. In circular turns with direction change, the 28-point max of 1.5 horses between riders applies.","Turns must be specified on the sketch sheet and the score sheet: the degrees to do (180, 270 and 360); the different degrees in a turn, with the 4 riders for each shown exactly where it happens; and 16 or 20-point turns done at the wall must be painted exactly at the place in the ring where the cuaje happens. If not, -2.","Peine (comb) (26): an exercise in groups of eight riders on parallel and opposite paths, where four pass facing the other four in an alternating way (not an overtake).","Carga de caballeria (cavalry charge) (26): a parallel formation of eight riders placed shoulder to shoulder, in one direction. It must meet its three times and keep the execution for three horses, and is penalised like any other exercise. It must be clear in order and design, and cannot form a 'V' or an inverted 'V'.","Combinado: a 28-point giro plus another exercise of any value, judged separately. They can be done in any order, both counted for the compulsory concepts. The design of any exercise of the routine cannot repeat in the two movements. A combinado with pair turns is not allowed. The 28-point giro must be done by all eight individually. The combinado drawing must be done with a nose-to-tail intersection at the entry of the exercise. Cross-giro: after the first intersection (nose-to-tail at entry) riders must go one horse before the second exercise. Giro-cross: riders go half a horse after finishing the turn and before the intersection of the second exercise; the intersection counted is nose-to-tail at entry. Giro-giro: half a horse between the first and the second turn. Starting the second exercise before finishing the first as described costs -4 for no advance of the combinado. Starting the second exercise after one and a half horses and up to two horses gives the first exercise the defect 'abierto'. If the second exercise is designed or done more than two horses away, it is not a combinado: they are judged separately and the team gets -25 for not meeting the Compulsory Routine. Definition of freeing the intersection: there must be a clear space between one mount and the other.","Libre (28): one cross, or ladder, or coladera only. A fourth concept can be repeated, but not its design: the design must differ even if the concept is the same. It must be marked as 'LIBRE' on the score and sketch sheets and gets 3 additional points, whether cross, coladera or ladder.","Compuesto (28): a compulsory concept that counts as a giro and cannot be used in the combinado. Four riders turn in pairs and four turn individually, with the eight riders taking part at the same time whatever the design, keeping up to 1.5 horses between riders as in 28-point exercises; beyond that is defect ABIERTO (A), progressively from distance to defect to disqualification. Speed is not penalised if it is reduced to do the turns, unless it falls to a trot or a walk. The turns of the compuesto go to the same side. Two different degrees can be used (180, 270, 360), with the same degrees among the individual riders and the same among the pairs. All eight start to turn at the same time (whether individual or pairs) and at the exit it is not a timing defect if individuals and pairs do not end equal, but it is if the 4 individuals do not end equal or the two pairs do not end together.","Compuesto bonus points depend on the degrees, adding the points for individual turns and for pair turns: for example 180+180 gives +6 and +6 = 12; 180 pairs and 270 individuals +6 and +8 = 14; 180 and 360 +6 and +10 = 16; 270 pairs and 180 individuals +8 and +6 = 14; 270 and 270 = 16; 270 and 360 = 18; 360 pairs and 180 individuals = 16; 360 and 270 = 18; 360 and 360 = 20. The judge writes the bonus on the score sheet and the captain only on her sketch sheet, adding correctly the bonus and the total of her routine.","Puntas (stopping demonstrations): the punta shows the rider's good rein on her horse. From a standing horse, the rider starts it at a walk (at most three steps) or gallop, a soft gallop that increases speed before 20 metres, or a long gallop from the start. The gallop stops on entering the rectangle, calling the mount with the rein, and the horse stops on its hindquarters in a complete stop. The rectangle must be clean and raked to see the marks left by the hindquarters. A team that does not arrive on time for its puntas is disqualified. Puntas beyond the number allowed disqualify the team.","The punta starts when the first punta rider enters the arena with her horse completely (the judge starts the timer) and ends when her horse leaves the rectangle completely by the front, after a complete stop. In arenas where the entry door is in the ring, riders go to the partidero and the judge starts timing when they cross 20 m walking to the back.","A rider can use a mount of another member of her team, or a horse outside the 8 that compete in the routine, in one or both puntas (Libre).","The captain or another member (if she is not riding the punta) can be in the ring only to verify the measure of the punta; she must stay outside the main rectangle at all times, on foot beside her team, approach when the male judges start to measure, then return. In the Libre category the person who checks the punta must do so on horseback. Entering the rectangle is -2 in the punta infractions box. The person checking cannot film the measure with any device. A rider who returns to check the measure disqualifies her punta; she must continue to the ring wall with her team. The punta is judged by the central judge with the support of a second judge at the partidero, and the male floor judge measures and decides the times.","Punta additional points: put the horse in hand, 1 point (at the partidero, held facing the rectangle, may move hands and legs without moving sideways or back and forth; at most 1.5 metres from the partidero wall (marked with a line); at most one minute; after two minutes with no release the penalty is -2 if the rectangle is clear and ready). Straight line, 1 point (gallop parallel to the lane from 10 metres until entering the rectangle). Speed, 1 point (from 20 metres until called with the rein; up to three steps allowed after putting in hand). Within the rectangle, 1 point (calling the horse within the lines; at least 6 metres of line within; no point if the rider leaves running out of the front of the rectangle). Pulling with one hand, 1 point (clearly from the start of calling; the stick can be held by both ends or one; the hand with the stick clearly apart from the rein hand; if both hands touch the rein or touch each other the point is lost). Greeting, 1 point (charro style, touching the hat brim with the back of the right hand, facing the public after the complete stop).","Punta drag: 6 metres in 1, 2 or 3 times gives 5, 4 or 3 points (4 times gives no points for times). Marks by the hindquarters are measured with a tape or odometer by an official FMCH judge, only the longest track of the same side inside the rectangle. If one leg marks 4 times, even if the other marks fewer, no points for times but points for the extra over 10 metres. If one marks 3 times and the other 2, the points for the larger number of times are given. More than 4 times: no points for times. If the first mark inside the rectangle is 50 cm or less it does not count as a time nor for measure. Drag over 10 metres: 1 point for 10 to 15.50 metres; 2 points for 15.51 metres or more.","Punta infractions: losing tack -2; trot -1; riding astride -4; taking the foot out of the stirrup -2; unnecessary punishment of the horse -4; a horse takes one limb out of the side lines of the rectangle after finishing the drag and stopping -4 (if it takes a limb out while dragging the punta is disqualified); the punta rider leaves the arena after having fully entered -6; the person who checks the punta enters the rectangle -2; stopping to watch the measure without being at the wall -4; instruction by the instructor or any outsider (except the optional stop and allowed categories) -4; if after two minutes the rider cannot release the mount in hand -2.","Punta disqualifications (keeping the infractions already made): not starting at the first attempt or if the horse goes back after the run started; starting without waiting for the ring to be clear of the rectangle area (within the sight of the rider); leaving the rectangle by a side while dragging; not dragging inside the rectangle; taking more than one limb out after dragging and before leaving the rectangle; not leaving by the front of the rectangle; refusal of the horse at any moment; the horse does not drag (does not put its feet in); the horse stops with its hands or picks; not stopping when called with the rein; a fall of the rider with or without the horse, including when she is left hanging by losing the seat; returning to the rectangle to check the measure, or approaching the male judges; the horse rearing at any time of the punta (when it clearly rests its hindquarters and lifts its hands from the middle upward); intervention of an instructor or any outsider at any time of the punta; making shadow or accompanying the punta rider in the arena, even by another rider of the team; the horse after the punta never makes a clear complete stop (not to confuse with the horse's spirit). A horse's refusal or rearing while dragging by its own momentum is not disqualified; if the horse finishes dragging and then rears, it is disqualified.","A horse that does not make a clear complete stop is not penalised if the horse is lively and takes sideways steps to the brow and the rider is clearly looking for the stop. It is penalised as no stop when the rider does not stop the mount and it advances forward after the punta.","Speed of the routine: -2 once per exercise (not per rider or occasion) when at the cuaje the team falls to a short/slow gallop or slows down (V); when one or several riders drop to a walk or stop (P); when one or several riders trot (T). The trot does not automatically mark speed; if a trot is marked and the speed also falls, both are marked. These are marked at any time of the routine except the entry. The only exception is the compuesto if speed is reduced for the turns, unless it falls to a trot or walk. In Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B and Charras Mayores speed rules are different (see each category).","Riding astride: -4 for each exercise where it happens and per rider, when a rider sits astride without getting off her mount, from entering the arena through the routine until the complete stop after the last exercise. Not applied while the timer is stopped in an optional stop if the rider is seated side-saddle when the timer restarts.","Loss of tack: -2 when any leather, textile, stick, extra accessory, clothing, adornment of the rider or horse falls, bursts or leaves its place, even if it hangs and does not fall. Exceptions: tearing of ruffles or lace, and a broken stick when the rider keeps a part in hand. Leg and hand protectors are only penalised when they fall completely or hang, not when a strap or buckle breaks without moving them. It is applied when it happens or when the judge notices it.","Fall with the horse: -2 at any moment, in preparation or execution. It is when the animal gives up the ribs, shoulder or belly (not a stumble), not caused by a collision. If the fall stops the performance, the stop is not penalised and the other infractions before the fall stay. If a fall with the horse happens in an exercise, the team can repeat the execution if not done by the eight, keeping all the infractions already made and marking those it repeats.","Departure of a member: -6 when any rider leaves the arena after having entered completely, at any time of the routine and during puntas. The timer does not stop and the penalties stay. Not applied if the caporal tells the team to leave for reasons not caused by it.","Unnecessary punishment: -4 each time a rider hits the horse's head and/or neck excessively with the stick, in any part of the routine including puntas (do not confuse with making shadow); also when she uses the spur or any other instrument to show evident anger at her horse without need.","Optional stop (paro opcional): no penalty; by decision of the team with the authorisation of the jury, justified when a rider or horse is in danger. Reasons: any part of the bit, chains, barbada, headstall or rein bursts; any part of the saddle, cinch, pecho-pretal, lead, bozalillo, bozal, bajador, hat, leg or hand accessories bursts; a horse or rider is clearly unfit to go on; the saddle clearly tilts; a saddle or tack used by the mount must be changed and puts the rider at risk (and it is no longer penalised as different tack because it was checked before); when the jury needs it because of dangerous riding or an outside situation. The timer stops until the routine restarts and no penalty is marked, except a rider not involved in the accident who uses the stop to be helped by an outsider: the team gets -6 as a performance stop.","Dangerous or inadequate riding: -2. Horses must not be hurt or drugged, nor riders drugged or drunk. Dangerous riding includes: riders or horses that lost consciousness, are fractured, visibly hurt or unable to use a part of the body in an accident and cannot go on; extreme speed without evident control; competing with a cast or splint without written medical authorisation (no penalty if a responsibility letter freeing the FMCH is given); riding with the foot out of the stirrup during the punta and/or any exercise or the full routine (marked per rider and per exercise).","Performance stop (paro de actuacion): -6, interrupting the presentation without justification and without the jury's authorisation, for example when a team makes a mistake in the routine and stops. The timer does not stop and what happens is penalised. Not penalised if the arena is invaded by a stranger, bull, mare or other animal, or by a collision or fall with or without a horse; then the timer is stopped.","Refusal and rearing (rehuse y paro de manos): -6. A horse refuses when it refuses an order or does not continue the direction of the execution insistently. Rearing is when the horse clearly rests its hindquarters and raises its hands from the middle of its body up, in any part of the routine. In the punta they disqualify it (see above).","Fall without the horse during the entry, cuaje and exit of exercises (three times): minus 100% of the exercise value (and the timer stops, only the affected riders can be helped; no performance stop or outsider intervention penalty). In the punta it disqualifies the punta. During the entry and exit of the routine and the preparation of exercises: -10 points.","Loss of seat during the entry, cuaje and exit of the exercise: -10 (when a rider loses contact with the seat and/or hangs, with or without a stop of the performance). During the entry or exit of the routine and the preparation of the exercise: -2.","Not all eight riders doing the exercise: minus 100% of the exercise value (and loses its additional points).","Rub (roce): -2 for a light contact without displacement. Contact with ruffles, hats, dresses and/or horse tails is not a rub. In the summary, -2 when two or more riders rub.","Collision in preparation, entry and/or exit of the routine: -10 points when contact of two or more horses causes: an impact with a notable consequence such as displacement and/or loss of continuity (change of gallop rhythm, a sideways movement, a stumble or trot after the impact); a notable demonstrable impact; riders falling with or without horses; a performance stop; loss of continuity of the preparation; a collision at the entry in a preparatory exercise before the routine begins; any horse crashed on the wall anywhere in the arena or lane at any time. NOT a collision: hand-to-leg contact without consequence (the horse does not move its limb), hat rubs and stirrup rubs.","Collision in execution (entry, cuaje and/or exit of the exercise): minus 100% of the value of the exercise (and its additional points) when the contact causes an impact with notable consequence, a notable impact, a stop of the performance, riders falling, a non-executed exercise because of the collision, or in pair turns contact with displacement and the riders or horses are caught (enganche: bodies physically joined, momentary, making separation hard) or stuck, losing continuity. The judge keeps marking the exercise after a collision. If the collision is not proved on video it is removed; if it is, the penalties marked are evaluated up to the moment it happened.","Intervention of an outsider: minus 100% of the exercise value and of the punta. It is when the trainer or anyone else gives instructions, or has direct physical and/or verbal contact with any member of the escaramuza at any moment of the punta (from entering the partidero to the end of the punta) or the routine (entry, exercises and exit) while the time runs and from any place, by shouts, signs, whistles, electronic devices, or passing a stick or any attire or tack. Verbal contact of the rider who gives the punta with the public is allowed. In Dientes de Leche, Infantil A and Infantil B the rules allow instructors as described in each category.","Not meeting the Compulsory Routine: -25 points for missing a value, a compulsory concept, doing any concept more than three times, or advancing more than two horses to do the second exercise of the combinado.","Entry or exit of exercises missing: -2 for exercises of any value and concept that do not keep one and a half horses of distance at the entries and/or exits in a straight line before the execution and one and a half horses after leaving the exercise; also when in coladera circles they do not run a horse and a half before and after the cuaje and/or undo the circle right after the cross.","Change of direction not met: -6 (see direction change rules). Not marking turns, degrees, two times and/or direction change: -2 each concept (degrees to do, turns at the wall or the 'bocamanga', and the direction change path with red ink including the exact cuaje moment).","Distance in execution: -2 points for each distance made at entry, execution and exit of any value as often as it occurs and for each rider who makes it, as long as it does not exceed the margin allowed to mark an execution defect. The offset of mounts in pairs in 20-point ladders (up to the horn of the partner) causes no distance (this applies only to ladders).","Execution defects in turns: -4 each: ABIERTO (A) when the limits of distance are exceeded at any moment of the turn, or the gap opens by more than 1.5 horses compared with the other riders, including after the turn started; LATERAL (L) when two partners in a pair turn have more than half a horse lateral; DISPLACEMENT (Z) when the mounts do not turn supporting the hindquarters, if the horse picks its hands at any point of the turn, or moves its hands/feet, such as a visible 'triangle' with its feet or 'caracoleando', moves back, or takes a lateral step with the opposite hand at the start; GRADES (G) when one or more riders do not complete their marked degrees (180, 270 and/or 360). Displacement in a pair turn is when one mount supports its hindquarters and the other goes around her in a circle. NOT displacement: dragging before starting the turn; pivoting or lifting a foot on its own axis; pushing off with the opposite foot to the support at the exit. If the horse does not support hands or feet and makes a full circle, the exercise is disqualified.","Execution defects in other exercises (not turns): -6 points for crosses, coladeras and all other linear exercises that have the error of one horse of timing (D). The defect 'abierto' (A) is marked for a distance greater than 1.5 horses. 16 points: more than 3 horses of distance in any of its gaps (abierto); coladera circle opened more than 3 horses; timing of one horse; circular groupings not completing a turn after formed (timing); parallel or diagonal groupings with more than one horse lateral (timing). 20 points: more than 2 horses in any gap; ladders in 2 times with the minimum distance of half a horse between times missing: -6 for timing defect; ladders in 2 times over 3 horses between times; simultaneous ladders more than 2 horses between blocks; 20-point coladeras in 2 times over 4 horses; coladera circle open more than 1.5 horses; timing of one horse. 26 and 28 points: more than 1.5 horses in any gap or coladera circle; timing of one horse. Also -6 for: abanico not completing a turn once formed or half abanicos not completing half a turn; groupings or combs or cavalry charges with more than one horse of lateral distance; flowers with timing over one horse or gaps over limits; coladeras of 26 points in two circles where the main circle has more than 1.5 horses; ladders with the distances between blocks over the limits.","General defects in execution: any exercise whose gaps between pairs or riders (forward, back or lateral) do not respect the margins of the value (except the distances between blocks of 4 and 4); more than one preparatory lap in any exercise; not marking preparations where there are more than 6 riders grouped or crossing. Walking in any turn: -4 (one or more riders move walking) (Libre, Juvenil, Infantil B and Charras Mayores).","Turns of less than 180 degrees: if because of an execution error one or more riders turn less than 180 degrees in 180-degree turns, -6. Doing a turn at a walk by any rider, -4 per turn exercise. Turn as a circle without supporting the hindquarters, or turning past tails in pair turns, disqualifies the exercise. Not completing at least 180 degrees in any turn, individual or pair: -6.","Bonus (additional) points: they are given to the 28-point exercises named below for being the most difficult, if they meet these requirements: linear exercises (except turns) must be prepared from the wall to the centre of the ring, all eight riders with no exercise in between, directly from the wall to the place of execution. In coladeras, a preparatory exercise to form the circle is allowed in which 4 riders stay forming the circle and 4 riders go to the wall to return to their intersection; it must be marked as a preparation. For turn and compuesto exercises, when an exercise is done before the entry of the turn it must be drawn as a preparation on the sketch sheet and marked 'P' on the score sheet.","Bonus table: coladera +3; ladder +3; cross +3; libre +3; giro of 180 degrees +6; 270 degrees +8; 360 degrees +10 (individual or pair turns); compuesto +6+8+10 / +6+8+10 according to the degrees of the pairs and individuals. If turns of different degrees are done in the same exercise, the bonus of the smaller degree of the exercise is given, except the compuesto. An execution defect does not lose the bonus. Not completing at least 180 degrees: -6.","In the combinado, bonus points: to the first exercise if it is 28 points and all eight riders leave the wall; to the second exercise if it is 28 points; when both are 28 points combined with turns, whatever the order. They are lost when one or more riders do not free the intersection of the first exercise to do the second. In a cross-giro combinado the bonus of the turns is lost; in a giro-giro combinado the bonus of the turns with more degrees is lost.","Disqualification of the routine: not presenting the 12 exercises (6 in Dientes de Leche); clapping, holding hands, taking off the hat, patting the mount or the saddle during the abanico; a base sum greater than the maximum of the category (Libre 304; Dientes de Leche 118; Infantil A 256; Infantil B 296; Juvenil 304; Charras Mayores 296).","Disqualification of exercises (the penalties up to then stay and the bonus is lost): executing the same exercise twice; executing equal the two exercises of the combinado (the second is disqualified); not doing an exercise listed on the sheet; changing the order of the exercises relative to the drawings; doing an exercise different from the drawing; the concept not matching the design; a collision during entry, cuaje or exit; intervention of an outsider during the exercise; doing any exercise in the lane of the arena; a fall without the horse during the entry, cuaje and exit; a turn of less than 180 degrees by any rider (in the Libre rule: doing a circle instead of a turn without supporting the hindquarters, or turning past tails in pair turns); in pair turns opening more than a horse lateral; an exercise done with fewer than eight riders; two exercises with the same design only inverting directions; doing the same concept more than three times (the fourth and further exercise is disqualified, except libre); grouping 3 or more riders at the precise moment of the cuaje even in different directions; in 26 and 28-point ladders crossing not through all the gaps or two horses nose to tail in the column; when a rider is left three horses from her team mates (except 16-point exercises).","Exceptions to disqualification of exercises: a stranger enters the ring without knowing the regulation; one or several charros do not leave or enter during the presentation and it is not caused by the escaramuza; someone enters the ring to pick up rubbish or an object that endangers riders; an outsider is needed in the optional stop; a bull, mare or other animal invades the ring; a door opens or closes and puts riders at risk. The presentation can be stopped without penalty and the judge stops the timer until it continues. Anyone can warn the team if riders are in danger.","Summary of penalties: -1: a rein without a button or knot at the centre; trot marked in the punta. -2: each different concept of attire or tack; each distance; when the team trots; when the team falls into a short or slow gallop (except in the compuesto); when two or more riders rub; a rider stops (paro de integrante); loss of tack; piercings on any visible part other than the earlobe; not entering and leaving the ring at the gallop; a missing chapeton on the saddle, headstall or pecho-pretal; the ring of the cinch and textile pecho-pretal on a gala saddle with hardware not the same material, concept and work as the hardware; not respecting one and a half horses at entries and exits; loss of seat in entry/exit of the routine or preparation; turns at the wall not marked; turns of same and different degrees not marked; two times (2T) not marked in the sketch; going over the minute to start; fall with the horse; not making a complete stop in the routine; the person assigned to check the punta enters the rectangle; dangerous or inadequate riding (foot out of the stirrup at any moment); not using a starched crinoline; going over the 2 minutes of putting in hand. -4: a horse takes a limb out of the side lines of the rectangle after stopping; riding astride in any moment of the routine or punta, per exercise and per rider; unnecessarily hitting the horse on the head; turn execution defect (180, 270, 360). -5: each minute or part over the allowed time; when the refutation process clarification does not succeed; riders leaving without greeting or without stopping at the end of the routine. -6: not handing in papers in time and form; a missing chapeta per rider; departure of a rider; performance stop; refusal or rearing in the routine; not passing the compulsory medical check; not respecting the practice time; not respecting the call, agreements and FMCH specifications; not arriving on time for tack check; execution defect in exercises of any value except turns; not marking preparations; not using traditional Mexican music; not meeting the characteristics of the direction change; not parading with the complete team (6 riders, with the 2 punta riders outside the lane); the punta rider leaves the arena after entering completely; execution defect in linear exercises. -10: a collision at the entry or exit of the routine or in the preparation of an exercise; clear contact of a horse against the wall anywhere in the arena at any time; loss of seat during entry, cuaje or exit of an exercise; a fall without the horse during the entry and exit of the routine and the preparation of an exercise. -25: a missing value, a missing compulsory concept, doing a concept more than three times, advancing more than two horses to do the second exercise of the combinado.","The additional rule of 2024-2028: if the calzonera comes out of the boot and leaves the leg bare, the team gets -2 in 'Infractions in presentation'. A rider with short calzoneras showing above the boot is disqualified."],"Allowed":["In the optional stop, an outsider can help the affected rider.","A spare vara can be carried on the saddle."],"NotAllowed":["Rock, pasodoble or any modernist or foreign music even with mariachi or banda, and personalised introductions of the team.","Clapping, holding hands, taking off the hat, or patting the horse or the saddle in the abanico.","A fourth repetition of a concept (except the libre) and any exercise done in the lane."],"Judging":["Each performance is scored by the official judges on official score sheets. At State Championships there are two judges; at the National Championship five judges score, and the highest and lowest scores are discarded.","Judges mark infractions on the sheet in the correct box. A penalty needs to be marked on the sheet and proved on video in the review to stand."],"Points":["Final score is the base value of the exercises, plus bonus points and the result of the puntas, minus the infractions."],"Notes":["The 2024-2028 regulation (edition February 2026) replaces the 2021-2024 regulation."]},"Markdown":"## About this competition\\n- A team of eight women riders on horseback shows 12 exercises (6 in Dientes de Leche) of the Compulsory Competition Routine at the gallop, plus puntas (stopping demonstrations), judged by official judges.\\n- Competition stages: terrain recognition; tack check; parade; puntas; entry of the routine; compulsory competition routine; exit of the routine; video review.\\n- An exercise is each one of the movements of the routine. Every exercise has three observation moments: entry (one and a half horses before the cuaje), cuaje (the exact moment of the crossing or turn, at the centre of the ring except 16 and 20-point exercises) and exit (one and a half horses after the cuaje).\\n\\n## Time and format\\n- Time of participation, puntas included: Libre 8 minutes with no tolerance; Dientes de Leche 15 minutes; Infantil A and B 11 minutes; Juvenil 8 minutes; Charras Mayores 11 minutes.\\n- The time starts when the first rider enters the arena and ends when, after the routine, the eight riders stop completely anywhere in the ring and greet in charro style. Overrun costs -5 points for each minute or part of a minute.\\n- The team has one minute to start its competition when the Sports Commission or the caporal says the terrain is ready, or -2. This is one minute for the puntas and one minute for the routine.\\n- The timer stops only for measuring the puntas and while the rectangle is cleaned. The time of each punta is added to the time of the routine.\\n\\n## Rules\\n- Terrain recognition: teams respect their practice times. All eight riders wear hats in practice. Teams cannot practice in the host arena at other times, or -6.\\n- Tack check (before the parade): attire and tack are checked, the original FMCH cards are compared with the copies on the sketch sheet and the riders are called by name. Teams sharing horses are not penalised: both teams come to the judges for the check, one team on foot and the other mounted; one of the teams must parade complete.\\n- Parade: the competing team parades with 6 riders at the start of the competition. The 2 riders who will ride the punta stay outside the lane of the ring. Not doing so is -6. If a rider or horse cannot parade because of an emergency just before the parade, the judge can allow an incomplete parade without penalty. Falls, hands up, refusals, collisions, trot or contact in the parade are never penalised.\\n- Entry of the routine: it starts when the first rider enters the arena completely and ends when the team starts the first exercise at the gallop after a complete stop (if not, the team is disqualified). The judge gives the entry signal to the Sports Commission. The eight riders enter grouped and gallop from 20 metres of the arena (-2 if not). They can do a preparatory exercise during the entry before the complete stop. Penalties during the entry while the timer is stopped are not applied (distances, execution defects, not keeping the coladera circle, no entry or exit, not marking preparations, 2 times, change of direction, exercise disqualifications).\\n- The Compulsory Competition Routine is the 12 exercises the team shows at the gallop (6 in Dientes de Leche). All eight riders must do all the exercises, showing clearly the entry, cuaje and exit of each one. An exercise done with fewer than 8 riders is disqualified (not all 8 are in the cuaje).\\n- As long as it meets the concepts and the values, the team can design the routine freely. It is drawn on both sheets, showing the place of each exercise in the ring.\\n- If the team does more than 12 exercises, the extra ones are marked as preparatory on the score sheet and drawn on the sketch sheet, and are judged as preparations with the penalties that happen in them. Preparations with more than 6 riders together before a centre exercise or an exercise showing a value, if not marked as 'preparacion', cost -6 each omitted. This does not apply to the final flourish of the routine.\\n- Music of the routine: traditional Mexican music played by mariachi, regional or banda. Rock, pasodoble or any modernist or foreign trend is forbidden, even with mariachi or banda, as is a personalised introduction of the team and music against the language and values of the charro sport. Not meeting this: -6 points.\\n- Exit of the routine: it ends when, after the last exercise, the eight riders stop completely and greet in charro style. Everything until then is judged. Leaving without greeting and/or stopping costs -5 points and the timer runs until they leave the arena. The whole team must leave at the gallop to the end of the partidero, or -2.\\n- The greeting is in charro style: touch the hat brim with the back of the right hand, facing the public, after a complete stop.\\n- Libre and Juvenil routine (12 exercises) needs at least one exercise of each compulsory concept: coladera, cruce, giro, escalera, abanico (26 points), combinado, libre and compuesto. Giros, coladeras, crosses, ladders and flowers can be repeated at most three times, counting the two exercises of the combinado and the compuesto. In the libre exercise a cruce, escalera or coladera can be repeated as a fourth concept, marked as 'libre' on the score sheet.\\n- Values: one exercise of 16 points; two of 20; two of 26 (one must be the abanico); five of 28 (including one compuesto and one libre); and one combinado (an independent compulsory concept of two exercises: a 28-point giro and a complementary exercise of any value 16, 20, 26 or 28, which must be made only of the compulsory concepts of the routine).\\n- The maximum base value of a Libre routine, before puntas and additional points, is 304. The combinado decides the base total: 28+28=304, 28+26=302, 28+20=296, 28+16=292. A base above 304 disqualifies the team (not presenting the compulsory routine). The maximum of additional points is 52: two individual 360-degree giros of 28 points, a compuesto of 28 points with 2 giros of 360 degrees, and four 28-point movements (ladders, coladeras, crosses or libre) with 3 additional points each.\\n- The concept of an exercise must match its design or the exercise is disqualified. The drawings are general designs to guide the execution and how it can be seen from outside.\\n- The initial value of the exercises is checked on the sketch sheets and confirmed at the execution. Designs must match on both sheets. If the design is the same as the correct sheet (either the sketch sheet or the score sheet) the exercise is not disqualified. If it matches neither, the exercise is disqualified when it happens.\\n- All exercises must be done by the eight riders, show entry, cuaje and exit clearly, and be simultaneous and/or with intersection. A direction change is allowed in all exercises except the combinado, the compuesto and coladeras (except the 26-point coladera), during the preparation of the exercise, keeping the same starting path during the entry of one and a half horses, the cuaje and the one and a half horse exit. The direction change counts from when the horse has fully changed its path with hands and feet. The moment and path of the change must be marked with red ink on the sketch and score sheet, or -2.\\n- In linear exercises, the riders must advance a horse and a half of linear path before reaching the nose-to-tail intersection of the exercise.\\n- Two exercises of the same design only with inverted directions, or the same design in another place in the ring, count as equal. Exercises with a perpendicular or diagonal intersection at the cuaje count as different.\\n- Maximum distances: 16-point exercises 3 horses (groupings: 1 horse between riders); 20-point exercises 2 horses (in two times, crosses and ladders 3 horses between the times, coladeras 4 horses); 26 and 28-point exercises 1.5 horses between one mount and another whatever the design; flowers, combs, abanico and cavalry charge, being groupings, 1 horse between riders; turns in pairs max half a horse lateral to the partner, and the gap must not open more than one horse lateral or the exercise is disqualified.\\n- 16-point exercises: a cross of two riders; turns of two by two riders; individual turns with max 3 horses between the eight; coladeras where riders cross one by one; flowers where two or three riders change direction at once; overtakes (rebases) or passes (pases) of 8 riders; groupings of four or eight; ladders where one rider crosses at a time.\\n- 20-point exercises: a cross of four riders; turns of four riders; coladeras where two riders cross at the same time; flowers where at least four riders change direction at once; ladders where two, three or four riders cross at once. 20-point exercises must have the same design in both times, even in a different place, moment or direction, or at the same time. The team must mark '2T' when done in two times, or -2.\\n- 26-point exercises: a cross of eight riders at once with two directions; coladeras where four crossing riders cross the circle at once, only entering or only exiting; the abanico, the cavalry charge and the comb; ladders with two directions as most where four riders cross at once.\\n- 28-point exercises: crosses with three directions or more; turns of eight riders at once; a coladera where four riders enter and exit the circle at the same time; ladders with more than two directions where four riders cross at once; libre with eight riders crossing at once; compuesto where four riders turn in pairs and four individually at once.\\n- In any exercise, if a rider is left at a distance of three horses from her team mates, or a gap opens between riders of more than three horses in the cuaje compared to the drawing, the exercise is disqualified (except 16-point exercises), and the penalties already made stay. If at the simultaneous intersection one or more riders have a separation of one horse, it is a timing defect (D). In any cuaje, an intersection at 1.5 horses or more at the edge of the exercise is disqualified because it leaves the rider outside the figure. The distance is judged progressively: distance, defect, disqualification.\\n- Groupings (16): formations where the 8 riders gather whatever the design, in entry, cuaje and exit: horizontal, parallel, diagonal, octagonal, half abanico or circular; when in blocks of 4 the maximum gap between blocks is one horse. Circular exercises must make at least one full turn once formed. Half abanicos must make at least half a turn of the ring once formed, with at most one horse lateral between riders and at most 3 horses between abanicos. Single files of 4 or 8 riders (parallel or opposite) are not groupings: they are preparations with no value.\\n- Pases and rebases (16): passes are movements of 8 riders passing in opposite directions (for example a tunnel, opposite passes). Rebase (overtake) are movements of 8 riders in the same direction from back to front. They must show the three times and be kept for three horses and are penalised like any exercise.\\n- Flowers (16 and 20): movements of eight riders with a radial entry or exit, from the edge to the centre or the other way, with a change of direction. Supporting on the hindquarters while changing direction disqualifies the exercise. All eight riders must be in the design at the execution. The drawing is made with a continuous line showing the start and end of the radial and, on the sketch sheet only, the path from the end of the radial to the exit point of every rider is dotted in red ink. A flower of 16 points has two or three riders changing direction at the same time; a flower of 20 points has at least four.\\n- Abanico (26): a diagonal exercise of eight riders where the leader goes along the ring wall. They must make one full turn of the ring once fully formed (8 riders). It keeps its traditional design. It is the only concept that has no entry and/or exit and only its execution is judged. Penalties for the same concept are not cumulative on the same rider (for example lateral distance, back, forward, trot, rub, defect).\\n- Crosses (16, 20, 26 and 28): movements with one or more intersections, done perpendicular and/or diagonally by two or more riders crossing at once. Value depends on the distance margins, the number of riders and the number of directions: 16 points two riders, distance up to 3 horses; 20 points four riders, up to 2 horses; 26 points eight riders in only two directions, up to 1.5 horses in each gap; 28 points eight riders in three or more directions, up to 1.5 horses in each gap. If three or more riders are grouped at the exact moment of the cuaje it is not a cross; it is an unspecified exercise, without value and disqualified (not if the group forms at the entry or exit).\\n- Ladders (16, 20, 26 and 28): exercises with a single column formed by a row going in one direction of two, three or four riders, which the other riders cross. All ladders must be drawn with the intersection at the same moment of the cuaje, in T or nose-to-tail. 16 points: cross one by one at the same time, with up to 3 horses between mounts, one column of two or four riders. 20 points: two or four riders cross at once, with at least 1.5 and at most 2 horses between the parallel, perpendicular or square columns (less than 1.5 horses disqualifies), one column of two, three or four riders, the same design. 26 points: all eight cross at once with up to 1.5 horses in two directions, in one column of four riders. 28 points: the columns of three or four riders go in one direction with up to 1.5 horses, crossed in two or more directions.\\n- In a ladder with a row of 4 riders, only the 2nd and 3rd can go in the column with mounts nose to tail, not the 2nd to the 1st or the 4th to the 3rd. In 26 and 28-point ladders no two horses can go nose to tail in the column, or the exercise is disqualified. The team must cross through all the gaps of the column. The offsets of mounts that go in pairs in 20-point ladders must be at most up to the horn of the partner and equal between them, or the 'distance' penalty. 16 or 20-point ladders can go one after another, parallel in the same direction or in opposite directions, even in different places in the ring, if the two have the same design. When done in two times, the team must mark '2T'. Between the first and second time there must be a minimum distance of half a horse (the first time must fully cross, freeing the hindquarters, and advance half a horse before the second crosses) and at most 3 horses.\\n- Coladeras (16, 20, 26 and 28): crosses where the main base is a circle. The circle must have four riders at the centre of the rectangle (except 16 and 20-point coladeras), while the other four cross it, entering and exiting, only entering or only exiting. Entry, cuaje and exit are linear in 16, 20 and 28-point coladeras. In 26-point coladeras the crossing riders can follow their circumference path and their entry or exit must be a straight line. Undoing the circle is done linearly. 16 points: cross the circle one by one with up to 3 horses between all mounts. 20 points: cross two by two with up to 1.5 horses distance in the circle. 26 points (only entry): four riders cross in each of the gaps of the circle when entering. 26 points (only exit): the circle is formed by the eight riders and four riders cross at the exit through each of the 4 gaps, in front of the next horse, and exit linearly. 26 points with two circles of 4 riders: all 8 follow the same path, and the cuaje is the 4 of the inner circle crossing the 4 of the outer circle through its 4 gaps. 28 points: four riders cross entering and exiting at the same time, linear entry, cuaje and exit. In 20, 26 and 28-point coladeras the maximum distance in the circle is 1.5 horses.\\n- In all coladeras the circle must be formed during one and a half horses before the cuaje and kept one and a half horses after it, with the distances kept in the three times; it cannot close, open or undo (-2). A 26-point coladera whose circle makes more than one full turn before the cuaje is a preparatory movement; if not marked as such, -6. Coladeras of any value without the entry and/or exit: -2.\\n- Giros (turns) (16, 20 and 28): exercises where the horses support their hindquarters to turn on their axis, keeping a radius equal to the length of the horse. The horse must keep the sequence and timing in the movement of its hands. It must not move back or sideways (displacement). 16 points: one by one, two by two, four by four or all at once with up to 3 horses distance. 20 points: four by four or all eight at once with up to 2 horses. 28 points: all eight at the same time, up to 1.5 horses.\\n- Only turns of 180, 270 and/or 360 degrees are allowed. Riders can turn individually or in four pairs. Combining individual and pair turns is forbidden in the same exercise, except in the compuesto. If turns of different degrees are done, only two variants of the three are allowed (180 with 270, 270 with 360 or 180 with 360), with 4 riders each; all 8 start to turn at the same time and the exit depends on the degrees done. Turns right and left at once are allowed only if the team marks with red and green on both sheets who turns left and right, only four riders to each side, and all riders turn the same degrees.\\n- In pair turns, riders can begin when they are parallel and their mounts are at least head to head of the partner's horse and at most when both are at tails. They end when they separate for the exit. The degrees start when the horses begin to turn. They do not need to be shoulder to shoulder. Distance in pair turns cannot be more than half a horse lateral with the partner at any time, or defect LATERAL (L); more than one horse lateral opens a disqualification. If the riders start to turn before being head to head with the partner, the team loses the bonus points for the degrees; if it looks like the turn is individual, not in pairs, it is disqualified.\\n- Giros with direction change must change direction in the preparation, keeping the same starting path, linear or circular, during the entry, cuaje and exit. All 8 riders with a change of direction turn the same degrees and the same side. Entry and exit paths cannot be combined (circular in, linear out) except in the 270-degree turn. The change can be made by eight, or by one block of four together; never individually. If paths are combined by the eight, exactly 4 riders change linearly and 4 circularly (never 3 and 5, 6 and 2, and so on). In pair turns with a change of direction, there are four pairs, and the change and the exit must be linear. In 180 and 360-degree turns, the path of the change is done the same way in entry and exit. In 270 turns a circular entry can combine with a linear exit, or both linear. A circle formed to prepare the turn cannot advance more than half a turn after the change, or more than half a turn after the turn. A linear change cannot advance more than three horses after the change. Not meeting these costs -6 for the change of direction plus -2 for entry and/or -2 for exit. In circular turns with direction change, the 28-point max of 1.5 horses between riders applies.\\n- Turns must be specified on the sketch sheet and the score sheet: the degrees to do (180, 270 and 360); the different degrees in a turn, with the 4 riders for each shown exactly where it happens; and 16 or 20-point turns done at the wall must be painted exactly at the place in the ring where the cuaje happens. If not, -2.\\n- Peine (comb) (26): an exercise in groups of eight riders on parallel and opposite paths, where four pass facing the other four in an alternating way (not an overtake).\\n- Carga de caballeria (cavalry charge) (26): a parallel formation of eight riders placed shoulder to shoulder, in one direction. It must meet its three times and keep the execution for three horses, and is penalised like any other exercise. It must be clear in order and design, and cannot form a 'V' or an inverted 'V'.\\n- Combinado: a 28-point giro plus another exercise of any value, judged separately. They can be done in any order, both counted for the compulsory concepts. The design of any exercise of the routine cannot repeat in the two movements. A combinado with pair turns is not allowed. The 28-point giro must be done by all eight individually. The combinado drawing must be done with a nose-to-tail intersection at the entry of the exercise. Cross-giro: after the first intersection (nose-to-tail at entry) riders must go one horse before the second exercise. Giro-cross: riders go half a horse after finishing the turn and before the intersection of the second exercise; the intersection counted is nose-to-tail at entry. Giro-giro: half a horse between the first and the second turn. Starting the second exercise before finishing the first as described costs -4 for no advance of the combinado. Starting the second exercise after one and a half horses and up to two horses gives the first exercise the defect 'abierto'. If the second exercise is designed or done more than two horses away, it is not a combinado: they are judged separately and the team gets -25 for not meeting the Compulsory Routine. Definition of freeing the intersection: there must be a clear space between one mount and the other.\\n- Libre (28): one cross, or ladder, or coladera only. A fourth concept can be repeated, but not its design: the design must differ even if the concept is the same. It must be marked as 'LIBRE' on the score and sketch sheets and gets 3 additional points, whether cross, coladera or ladder.\\n- Compuesto (28): a compulsory concept that counts as a giro and cannot be used in the combinado. Four riders turn in pairs and four turn individually, with the eight riders taking part at the same time whatever the design, keeping up to 1.5 horses between riders as in 28-point exercises; beyond that is defect ABIERTO (A), progressively from distance to defect to disqualification. Speed is not penalised if it is reduced to do the turns, unless it falls to a trot or a walk. The turns of the compuesto go to the same side. Two different degrees can be used (180, 270, 360), with the same degrees among the individual riders and the same among the pairs. All eight start to turn at the same time (whether individual or pairs) and at the exit it is not a timing defect if individuals and pairs do not end equal, but it is if the 4 individuals do not end equal or the two pairs do not end together.\\n- Compuesto bonus points depend on the degrees, adding the points for individual turns and for pair turns: for example 180+180 gives +6 and +6 = 12; 180 pairs and 270 individuals +6 and +8 = 14; 180 and 360 +6 and +10 = 16; 270 pairs and 180 individuals +8 and +6 = 14; 270 and 270 = 16; 270 and 360 = 18; 360 pairs and 180 individuals = 16; 360 and 270 = 18; 360 and 360 = 20. The judge writes the bonus on the score sheet and the captain only on her sketch sheet, adding correctly the bonus and the total of her routine.\\n- Puntas (stopping demonstrations): the punta shows the rider's good rein on her horse. From a standing horse, the rider starts it at a walk (at most three steps) or gallop, a soft gallop that increases speed before 20 metres, or a long gallop from the start. The gallop stops on entering the rectangle, calling the mount with the rein, and the horse stops on its hindquarters in a complete stop. The rectangle must be clean and raked to see the marks left by the hindquarters. A team that does not arrive on time for its puntas is disqualified. Puntas beyond the number allowed disqualify the team.\\n- The punta starts when the first punta rider enters the arena with her horse completely (the judge starts the timer) and ends when her horse leaves the rectangle completely by the front, after a complete stop. In arenas where the entry door is in the ring, riders go to the partidero and the judge starts timing when they cross 20 m walking to the back.\\n- A rider can use a mount of another member of her team, or a horse outside the 8 that compete in the routine, in one or both puntas (Libre).\\n- The captain or another member (if she is not riding the punta) can be in the ring only to verify the measure of the punta; she must stay outside the main rectangle at all times, on foot beside her team, approach when the male judges start to measure, then return. In the Libre category the person who checks the punta must do so on horseback. Entering the rectangle is -2 in the punta infractions box. The person checking cannot film the measure with any device. A rider who returns to check the measure disqualifies her punta; she must continue to the ring wall with her team. The punta is judged by the central judge with the support of a second judge at the partidero, and the male floor judge measures and decides the times.\\n- Punta additional points: put the horse in hand, 1 point (at the partidero, held facing the rectangle, may move hands and legs without moving sideways or back and forth; at most 1.5 metres from the partidero wall (marked with a line); at most one minute; after two minutes with no release the penalty is -2 if the rectangle is clear and ready). Straight line, 1 point (gallop parallel to the lane from 10 metres until entering the rectangle). Speed, 1 point (from 20 metres until called with the rein; up to three steps allowed after putting in hand). Within the rectangle, 1 point (calling the horse within the lines; at least 6 metres of line within; no point if the rider leaves running out of the front of the rectangle). Pulling with one hand, 1 point (clearly from the start of calling; the stick can be held by both ends or one; the hand with the stick clearly apart from the rein hand; if both hands touch the rein or touch each other the point is lost). Greeting, 1 point (charro style, touching the hat brim with the back of the right hand, facing the public after the complete stop).\\n- Punta drag: 6 metres in 1, 2 or 3 times gives 5, 4 or 3 points (4 times gives no points for times). Marks by the hindquarters are measured with a tape or odometer by an official FMCH judge, only the longest track of the same side inside the rectangle. If one leg marks 4 times, even if the other marks fewer, no points for times but points for the extra over 10 metres. If one marks 3 times and the other 2, the points for the larger number of times are given. More than 4 times: no points for times. If the first mark inside the rectangle is 50 cm or less it does not count as a time nor for measure. Drag over 10 metres: 1 point for 10 to 15.50 metres; 2 points for 15.51 metres or more.\\n- Punta infractions: losing tack -2; trot -1; riding astride -4; taking the foot out of the stirrup -2; unnecessary punishment of the horse -4; a horse takes one limb out of the side lines of the rectangle after finishing the drag and stopping -4 (if it takes a limb out while dragging the punta is disqualified); the punta rider leaves the arena after having fully entered -6; the person who checks the punta enters the rectangle -2; stopping to watch the measure without being at the wall -4; instruction by the instructor or any outsider (except the optional stop and allowed categories) -4; if after two minutes the rider cannot release the mount in hand -2.\\n- Punta disqualifications (keeping the infractions already made): not starting at the first attempt or if the horse goes back after the run started; starting without waiting for the ring to be clear of the rectangle area (within the sight of the rider); leaving the rectangle by a side while dragging; not dragging inside the rectangle; taking more than one limb out after dragging and before leaving the rectangle; not leaving by the front of the rectangle; refusal of the horse at any moment; the horse does not drag (does not put its feet in); the horse stops with its hands or picks; not stopping when called with the rein; a fall of the rider with or without the horse, including when she is left hanging by losing the seat; returning to the rectangle to check the measure, or approaching the male judges; the horse rearing at any time of the punta (when it clearly rests its hindquarters and lifts its hands from the middle upward); intervention of an instructor or any outsider at any time of the punta; making shadow or accompanying the punta rider in the arena, even by another rider of the team; the horse after the punta never makes a clear complete stop (not to confuse with the horse's spirit). A horse's refusal or rearing while dragging by its own momentum is not disqualified; if the horse finishes dragging and then rears, it is disqualified.\\n- A horse that does not make a clear complete stop is not penalised if the horse is lively and takes sideways steps to the brow and the rider is clearly looking for the stop. It is penalised as no stop when the rider does not stop the mount and it advances forward after the punta.\\n- Speed of the routine: -2 once per exercise (not per rider or occasion) when at the cuaje the team falls to a short/slow gallop or slows down (V); when one or several riders drop to a walk or stop (P); when one or several riders trot (T). The trot does not automatically mark speed; if a trot is marked and the speed also falls, both are marked. These are marked at any time of the routine except the entry. The only exception is the compuesto if speed is reduced for the turns, unless it falls to a trot or walk. In Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B and Charras Mayores speed rules are different (see each category).\\n- Riding astride: -4 for each exercise where it happens and per rider, when a rider sits astride without getting off her mount, from entering the arena through the routine until the complete stop after the last exercise. Not applied while the timer is stopped in an optional stop if the rider is seated side-saddle when the timer restarts.\\n- Loss of tack: -2 when any leather, textile, stick, extra accessory, clothing, adornment of the rider or horse falls, bursts or leaves its place, even if it hangs and does not fall. Exceptions: tearing of ruffles or lace, and a broken stick when the rider keeps a part in hand. Leg and hand protectors are only penalised when they fall completely or hang, not when a strap or buckle breaks without moving them. It is applied when it happens or when the judge notices it.\\n- Fall with the horse: -2 at any moment, in preparation or execution. It is when the animal gives up the ribs, shoulder or belly (not a stumble), not caused by a collision. If the fall stops the performance, the stop is not penalised and the other infractions before the fall stay. If a fall with the horse happens in an exercise, the team can repeat the execution if not done by the eight, keeping all the infractions already made and marking those it repeats.\\n- Departure of a member: -6 when any rider leaves the arena after having entered completely, at any time of the routine and during puntas. The timer does not stop and the penalties stay. Not applied if the caporal tells the team to leave for reasons not caused by it.\\n- Unnecessary punishment: -4 each time a rider hits the horse's head and/or neck excessively with the stick, in any part of the routine including puntas (do not confuse with making shadow); also when she uses the spur or any other instrument to show evident anger at her horse without need.\\n- Optional stop (paro opcional): no penalty; by decision of the team with the authorisation of the jury, justified when a rider or horse is in danger. Reasons: any part of the bit, chains, barbada, headstall or rein bursts; any part of the saddle, cinch, pecho-pretal, lead, bozalillo, bozal, bajador, hat, leg or hand accessories bursts; a horse or rider is clearly unfit to go on; the saddle clearly tilts; a saddle or tack used by the mount must be changed and puts the rider at risk (and it is no longer penalised as different tack because it was checked before); when the jury needs it because of dangerous riding or an outside situation. The timer stops until the routine restarts and no penalty is marked, except a rider not involved in the accident who uses the stop to be helped by an outsider: the team gets -6 as a performance stop.\\n- Dangerous or inadequate riding: -2. Horses must not be hurt or drugged, nor riders drugged or drunk. Dangerous riding includes: riders or horses that lost consciousness, are fractured, visibly hurt or unable to use a part of the body in an accident and cannot go on; extreme speed without evident control; competing with a cast or splint without written medical authorisation (no penalty if a responsibility letter freeing the FMCH is given); riding with the foot out of the stirrup during the punta and/or any exercise or the full routine (marked per rider and per exercise).\\n- Performance stop (paro de actuacion): -6, interrupting the presentation without justification and without the jury's authorisation, for example when a team makes a mistake in the routine and stops. The timer does not stop and what happens is penalised. Not penalised if the arena is invaded by a stranger, bull, mare or other animal, or by a collision or fall with or without a horse; then the timer is stopped.\\n- Refusal and rearing (rehuse y paro de manos): -6. A horse refuses when it refuses an order or does not continue the direction of the execution insistently. Rearing is when the horse clearly rests its hindquarters and raises its hands from the middle of its body up, in any part of the routine. In the punta they disqualify it (see above).\\n- Fall without the horse during the entry, cuaje and exit of exercises (three times): minus 100% of the exercise value (and the timer stops, only the affected riders can be helped; no performance stop or outsider intervention penalty). In the punta it disqualifies the punta. During the entry and exit of the routine and the preparation of exercises: -10 points.\\n- Loss of seat during the entry, cuaje and exit of the exercise: -10 (when a rider loses contact with the seat and/or hangs, with or without a stop of the performance). During the entry or exit of the routine and the preparation of the exercise: -2.\\n- Not all eight riders doing the exercise: minus 100% of the exercise value (and loses its additional points).\\n- Rub (roce): -2 for a light contact without displacement. Contact with ruffles, hats, dresses and/or horse tails is not a rub. In the summary, -2 when two or more riders rub.\\n- Collision in preparation, entry and/or exit of the routine: -10 points when contact of two or more horses causes: an impact with a notable consequence such as displacement and/or loss of continuity (change of gallop rhythm, a sideways movement, a stumble or trot after the impact); a notable demonstrable impact; riders falling with or without horses; a performance stop; loss of continuity of the preparation; a collision at the entry in a preparatory exercise before the routine begins; any horse crashed on the wall anywhere in the arena or lane at any time. NOT a collision: hand-to-leg contact without consequence (the horse does not move its limb), hat rubs and stirrup rubs.\\n- Collision in execution (entry, cuaje and/or exit of the exercise): minus 100% of the value of the exercise (and its additional points) when the contact causes an impact with notable consequence, a notable impact, a stop of the performance, riders falling, a non-executed exercise because of the collision, or in pair turns contact with displacement and the riders or horses are caught (enganche: bodies physically joined, momentary, making separation hard) or stuck, losing continuity. The judge keeps marking the exercise after a collision. If the collision is not proved on video it is removed; if it is, the penalties marked are evaluated up to the moment it happened.\\n- Intervention of an outsider: minus 100% of the exercise value and of the punta. It is when the trainer or anyone else gives instructions, or has direct physical and/or verbal contact with any member of the escaramuza at any moment of the punta (from entering the partidero to the end of the punta) or the routine (entry, exercises and exit) while the time runs and from any place, by shouts, signs, whistles, electronic devices, or passing a stick or any attire or tack. Verbal contact of the rider who gives the punta with the public is allowed. In Dientes de Leche, Infantil A and Infantil B the rules allow instructors as described in each category.\\n- Not meeting the Compulsory Routine: -25 points for missing a value, a compulsory concept, doing any concept more than three times, or advancing more than two horses to do the second exercise of the combinado.\\n- Entry or exit of exercises missing: -2 for exercises of any value and concept that do not keep one and a half horses of distance at the entries and/or exits in a straight line before the execution and one and a half horses after leaving the exercise; also when in coladera circles they do not run a horse and a half before and after the cuaje and/or undo the circle right after the cross.\\n- Change of direction not met: -6 (see direction change rules). Not marking turns, degrees, two times and/or direction change: -2 each concept (degrees to do, turns at the wall or the 'bocamanga', and the direction change path with red ink including the exact cuaje moment).\\n- Distance in execution: -2 points for each distance made at entry, execution and exit of any value as often as it occurs and for each rider who makes it, as long as it does not exceed the margin allowed to mark an execution defect. The offset of mounts in pairs in 20-point ladders (up to the horn of the partner) causes no distance (this applies only to ladders).\\n- Execution defects in turns: -4 each: ABIERTO (A) when the limits of distance are exceeded at any moment of the turn, or the gap opens by more than 1.5 horses compared with the other riders, including after the turn started; LATERAL (L) when two partners in a pair turn have more than half a horse lateral; DISPLACEMENT (Z) when the mounts do not turn supporting the hindquarters, if the horse picks its hands at any point of the turn, or moves its hands/feet, such as a visible 'triangle' with its feet or 'caracoleando', moves back, or takes a lateral step with the opposite hand at the start; GRADES (G) when one or more riders do not complete their marked degrees (180, 270 and/or 360). Displacement in a pair turn is when one mount supports its hindquarters and the other goes around her in a circle. NOT displacement: dragging before starting the turn; pivoting or lifting a foot on its own axis; pushing off with the opposite foot to the support at the exit. If the horse does not support hands or feet and makes a full circle, the exercise is disqualified.\\n- Execution defects in other exercises (not turns): -6 points for crosses, coladeras and all other linear exercises that have the error of one horse of timing (D). The defect 'abierto' (A) is marked for a distance greater than 1.5 horses. 16 points: more than 3 horses of distance in any of its gaps (abierto); coladera circle opened more than 3 horses; timing of one horse; circular groupings not completing a turn after formed (timing); parallel or diagonal groupings with more than one horse lateral (timing). 20 points: more than 2 horses in any gap; ladders in 2 times with the minimum distance of half a horse between times missing: -6 for timing defect; ladders in 2 times over 3 horses between times; simultaneous ladders more than 2 horses between blocks; 20-point coladeras in 2 times over 4 horses; coladera circle open more than 1.5 horses; timing of one horse. 26 and 28 points: more than 1.5 horses in any gap or coladera circle; timing of one horse. Also -6 for: abanico not completing a turn once formed or half abanicos not completing half a turn; groupings or combs or cavalry charges with more than one horse of lateral distance; flowers with timing over one horse or gaps over limits; coladeras of 26 points in two circles where the main circle has more than 1.5 horses; ladders with the distances between blocks over the limits.\\n- General defects in execution: any exercise whose gaps between pairs or riders (forward, back or lateral) do not respect the margins of the value (except the distances between blocks of 4 and 4); more than one preparatory lap in any exercise; not marking preparations where there are more than 6 riders grouped or crossing. Walking in any turn: -4 (one or more riders move walking) (Libre, Juvenil, Infantil B and Charras Mayores).\\n- Turns of less than 180 degrees: if because of an execution error one or more riders turn less than 180 degrees in 180-degree turns, -6. Doing a turn at a walk by any rider, -4 per turn exercise. Turn as a circle without supporting the hindquarters, or turning past tails in pair turns, disqualifies the exercise. Not completing at least 180 degrees in any turn, individual or pair: -6.\\n- Bonus (additional) points: they are given to the 28-point exercises named below for being the most difficult, if they meet these requirements: linear exercises (except turns) must be prepared from the wall to the centre of the ring, all eight riders with no exercise in between, directly from the wall to the place of execution. In coladeras, a preparatory exercise to form the circle is allowed in which 4 riders stay forming the circle and 4 riders go to the wall to return to their intersection; it must be marked as a preparation. For turn and compuesto exercises, when an exercise is done before the entry of the turn it must be drawn as a preparation on the sketch sheet and marked 'P' on the score sheet.\\n- Bonus table: coladera +3; ladder +3; cross +3; libre +3; giro of 180 degrees +6; 270 degrees +8; 360 degrees +10 (individual or pair turns); compuesto +6+8+10 / +6+8+10 according to the degrees of the pairs and individuals. If turns of different degrees are done in the same exercise, the bonus of the smaller degree of the exercise is given, except the compuesto. An execution defect does not lose the bonus. Not completing at least 180 degrees: -6.\\n- In the combinado, bonus points: to the first exercise if it is 28 points and all eight riders leave the wall; to the second exercise if it is 28 points; when both are 28 points combined with turns, whatever the order. They are lost when one or more riders do not free the intersection of the first exercise to do the second. In a cross-giro combinado the bonus of the turns is lost; in a giro-giro combinado the bonus of the turns with more degrees is lost.\\n- Disqualification of the routine: not presenting the 12 exercises (6 in Dientes de Leche); clapping, holding hands, taking off the hat, patting the mount or the saddle during the abanico; a base sum greater than the maximum of the category (Libre 304; Dientes de Leche 118; Infantil A 256; Infantil B 296; Juvenil 304; Charras Mayores 296).\\n- Disqualification of exercises (the penalties up to then stay and the bonus is lost): executing the same exercise twice; executing equal the two exercises of the combinado (the second is disqualified); not doing an exercise listed on the sheet; changing the order of the exercises relative to the drawings; doing an exercise different from the drawing; the concept not matching the design; a collision during entry, cuaje or exit; intervention of an outsider during the exercise; doing any exercise in the lane of the arena; a fall without the horse during the entry, cuaje and exit; a turn of less than 180 degrees by any rider (in the Libre rule: doing a circle instead of a turn without supporting the hindquarters, or turning past tails in pair turns); in pair turns opening more than a horse lateral; an exercise done with fewer than eight riders; two exercises with the same design only inverting directions; doing the same concept more than three times (the fourth and further exercise is disqualified, except libre); grouping 3 or more riders at the precise moment of the cuaje even in different directions; in 26 and 28-point ladders crossing not through all the gaps or two horses nose to tail in the column; when a rider is left three horses from her team mates (except 16-point exercises).\\n- Exceptions to disqualification of exercises: a stranger enters the ring without knowing the regulation; one or several charros do not leave or enter during the presentation and it is not caused by the escaramuza; someone enters the ring to pick up rubbish or an object that endangers riders; an outsider is needed in the optional stop; a bull, mare or other animal invades the ring; a door opens or closes and puts riders at risk. The presentation can be stopped without penalty and the judge stops the timer until it continues. Anyone can warn the team if riders are in danger.\\n- Summary of penalties: -1: a rein without a button or knot at the centre; trot marked in the punta. -2: each different concept of attire or tack; each distance; when the team trots; when the team falls into a short or slow gallop (except in the compuesto); when two or more riders rub; a rider stops (paro de integrante); loss of tack; piercings on any visible part other than the earlobe; not entering and leaving the ring at the gallop; a missing chapeton on the saddle, headstall or pecho-pretal; the ring of the cinch and textile pecho-pretal on a gala saddle with hardware not the same material, concept and work as the hardware; not respecting one and a half horses at entries and exits; loss of seat in entry/exit of the routine or preparation; turns at the wall not marked; turns of same and different degrees not marked; two times (2T) not marked in the sketch; going over the minute to start; fall with the horse; not making a complete stop in the routine; the person assigned to check the punta enters the rectangle; dangerous or inadequate riding (foot out of the stirrup at any moment); not using a starched crinoline; going over the 2 minutes of putting in hand. -4: a horse takes a limb out of the side lines of the rectangle after stopping; riding astride in any moment of the routine or punta, per exercise and per rider; unnecessarily hitting the horse on the head; turn execution defect (180, 270, 360). -5: each minute or part over the allowed time; when the refutation process clarification does not succeed; riders leaving without greeting or without stopping at the end of the routine. -6: not handing in papers in time and form; a missing chapeta per rider; departure of a rider; performance stop; refusal or rearing in the routine; not passing the compulsory medical check; not respecting the practice time; not respecting the call, agreements and FMCH specifications; not arriving on time for tack check; execution defect in exercises of any value except turns; not marking preparations; not using traditional Mexican music; not meeting the characteristics of the direction change; not parading with the complete team (6 riders, with the 2 punta riders outside the lane); the punta rider leaves the arena after entering completely; execution defect in linear exercises. -10: a collision at the entry or exit of the routine or in the preparation of an exercise; clear contact of a horse against the wall anywhere in the arena at any time; loss of seat during entry, cuaje or exit of an exercise; a fall without the horse during the entry and exit of the routine and the preparation of an exercise. -25: a missing value, a missing compulsory concept, doing a concept more than three times, advancing more than two horses to do the second exercise of the combinado.\\n- The additional rule of 2024-2028: if the calzonera comes out of the boot and leaves the leg bare, the team gets -2 in 'Infractions in presentation'. A rider with short calzoneras showing above the boot is disqualified.\\n\\n## Allowed\\n- In the optional stop, an outsider can help the affected rider.\\n- A spare vara can be carried on the saddle.\\n\\n## Not allowed\\n- Rock, pasodoble or any modernist or foreign music even with mariachi or banda, and personalised introductions of the team.\\n- Clapping, holding hands, taking off the hat, or patting the horse or the saddle in the abanico.\\n- A fourth repetition of a concept (except the libre) and any exercise done in the lane.\\n\\n## Judging\\n- Each performance is scored by the official judges on official score sheets. At State Championships there are two judges; at the National Championship five judges score, and the highest and lowest scores are discarded.\\n- Judges mark infractions on the sheet in the correct box. A penalty needs to be marked on the sheet and proved on video in the review to stand.\\n\\n## Points\\n- Final score is the base value of the exercises, plus bonus points and the result of the puntas, minus the infractions.\\n\\n## Notes for participants\\n- The 2024-2028 regulation (edition February 2026) replaces the 2021-2024 regulation."}]`),r=[],s=[{Key:"FmchWebsite",Kind:"link",Label:"FMCH official website",Value:"www.fmcharreria.com (the 2026 site is fmcharreria.org.mx)",Source:"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Categoria Libre, 2024-2028 (edition February 2026), Preambulo; FMCH website"},{Key:"RegulationOfficials",Kind:"contact",Label:"FMCH officers named in the regulation (no phone printed)",Value:{Name:"Juan Banuelos Ramirez (Secretary of Sport); Silvia Graciela Rodriguez Rodriguez (National Coordinator of Escaramuzas); Jose de Jesus Fermin Torres (National Coordinator of Judges)",Role:"Secretariat of Sport and National Coordination (Mexico City, February 2026)",Phone:"",WhatsApp:"",Email:""},Source:"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Categoria Libre, 2024-2028 (edition February 2026), Transitorios"},{Key:"MinimumStateScore",Kind:"text",Label:"Minimum state score for a National pass",Value:"290 points",Source:"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras, Categoria Libre, 2024-2028 (edition February 2026), Art. 10, 15, 18, 19"},{Key:"Announcements",Kind:"news",Label:"Notices on the FMCH website",Value:[{Date:"",Title:"2026 Official Regulation for Escaramuzas and Damas Charras",Text:"The FMCH publishes the 2024-2028 regulation, adapted in February 2026, as two documents: Libre category, and Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil and Charras Mayores.",Url:"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/reglamentos/reglamento-oficial-para-escaramuzas-2026/"},{Date:"",Title:"2026 score and sketch sheets",Text:"Score sheets and sketch sheets for Escaramuzas Libre and Mayor 2026, and for Infantil escaramuzas 2026, are on the FMCH site.",Url:"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/sector-femenil/formato-femenil/hoja-de-calificacion-para-escaramuzas-2026/"},{Date:"",Title:"LXXXII Congress and National Charro Championship, San Luis Potosi 2026",Text:"The FMCH site lists the official programme, results and classification, the official list of classified teams, the official list of escaramuzas and a summary of escaramuza sanctions for the 2026 National Championship.",Url:"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/"}],Source:"FMCH website, 2026"}],h=[{Claim:"Escaramuza Charra is a separate women's team competition of Mexican charreria, governed by the FMCH Official Regulation for Escaramuzas and Damas Charras. The current text is the 2024-2028 edition adapted in February 2026, in two documents: Libre category, and Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil and Charras Mayores.",SourceType:"Primary - organiser's own circular",Source:"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras 2024-2028 (Libre)",Url:"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/wp-content/uploads/REGLAMENTO-DE-ESCARAMUZAS-LIBRE-2024-2028-B.pdf"},{Claim:"Categories Dientes de Leche, Infantil A, Infantil B, Juvenil and Charras Mayores (45 years and older), with category rules for times, puntas, base values and instructors.",SourceType:"Primary - organiser's own circular",Source:"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras 2024-2028 (other categories)",Url:"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/wp-content/uploads/REGLAMENTO-PARA-ESCARAMUZAS-Y-DAMAS-CHARRAS-CAT.-DL-IAIBJUVCHM-2024-2028-B.pdf"},{Claim:"The earlier 2021-2024 Libre regulation (February 2022), cited by the template, was read and compared; it is replaced by the 2024-2028 text.",SourceType:"Primary - organiser's own circular",Source:"FMCH Reglamento Oficial para Escaramuzas y Damas Charras 2021-2024 (Libre)",Url:"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/wp-content/uploads/REGLAMENTO-DE-ESCARAMUZAS-2021-2024.pdf"},{Claim:"Score sheets (hojas de calificacion) and sketch sheets for Escaramuzas 2026.",SourceType:"Primary - organiser's own circular",Source:"FMCH formats for Escaramuzas 2026",Url:"https://fmcharreria.org.mx/sector-femenil/formato-femenil/hoja-de-calificacion-para-escaramuzas-2026/"}],l={TemplateKey:e,Year:2026,Note:t,Page:a,ParticipantTypes:o,AgeGroups:n,Competitions:i,CompetitionAgeGroups:r,Variables:s,Evidence:h};export{n as AgeGroups,r as CompetitionAgeGroups,i as Competitions,h as Evidence,t as Note,a as Page,o as ParticipantTypes,e as TemplateKey,s as Variables,d as Year,l as default};
